cess probability implied by our bull case (20%) is **below** 1.5\u00d7 base rates \u2014 no red flag. The bear base rate (dividend cut 40\u201350% when FCF coverage <100%) is a real concern given a 112% payout ratio [Estimate].\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n**Step 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences):**\nKarat Packaging designs, sources, and distributes disposable foodservice packaging \u2014 cups, lids, containers, cutlery, bags \u2014 primarily to restaurants, chains, and distributors across the U.S. It differentiates via an import-led sourcing model (Taiwan/China/domestic), an owned logistics network including local delivery fleets and warehouses, and a growing eco-friendly line (KaratEarth). The company's strategy is shifting from pure distribution to a hybrid sourcing + direct e-commerce model (18.9% of sales online, growing 23.6% y/y). \n**Understandability: High.** This is a straightforward industrial distribution business. No analysis downgrade.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat assessment:**\n\n| Moat type | Score (0-3) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (brand) | 1 | Stable \u2014 Karat/KaratEarth brand growing but not dominant |\n| Switching costs | 1 | Moderate \u2014 chain relationships stickier than independents |\n| Network effects | 0 | None |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Widening \u2014 Taiwan/US sourcing mix lowered landed cost vs China; but freight/resin countervail |\n| Economies of scale | 1 | Narrowing \u2014 smaller than Pactiv/Dart; local delivery fleet helps only regionally |\n| Regulatory barriers | 1 | Improving \u2014 eco-regulation forces SKU proliferation, favors nimble importers |\n| Data moat | 1 | Stable \u2014 e-commerce sales data informs SKU and sourcing decisions |\n| **Total** | **7 / 21 \u2192 Narrow Moat** | Direction: **Stable-to-slightly-widening** (paper bag expansion, e-comm analytics) |\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch classification:** **Fast Grower** (10%+ growth, small-cap, sub-sector share gains). Checkpoints: (i) growth sustainability \u2014 supported by chain adds + e-comm, challenged by macro; (ii) balance sheet \u2014 clean, no net debt; (iii) valuation \u2014 fails Lynch's PEG < 1.5 test: PEG \u2248 36\u00d7 normalized / 8 = 4.5.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial strength scorecard:**\n\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR (5y) | +9.6% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Latest quarterly rev growth | +9.9\u201312.9% | [Actual] |\n| Gross margin, FY2025 | 36.8% | [Actual] |\n| Operating margin, FY2025 | 8.8% (median 6yr: 8.9%) | [Actual] |\n| Net margin, FY2025 | 6.6% | [Actual] |\n| ROE | 30.6% | [Actual \u2014 vendor] |\n| ROIC (NOPAT/IC) | \u224819\u201320% | [Estimate] |\n| FCF/OCF conversion | $33M / $34M = 97% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Net debt / EBITDA | \u2248 0.0\u00d7 (net cash \u2248 $1M) | [Estimate] |\n| Interest coverage | >15\u00d7 (EBIT $41M \u00b1 interest) | [Estimate] |\n\n**Judgment: Good.** Clean balance sheet, high ROIC, low capex; but margins are thin (8.8% op) and freight/resin are persistent squeezes.\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0\u201315):**\n\n| Item | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 1 | Repurchases $2M/quarter at \u2248$27\u201328 average; below current price is decent; but SBC dilution offset (net shares +0.3%/yr; 3-yr CAGR +5.5%) |\n| M&A track record | 1 | Limited history; acquisitions not material |\n| Capex efficiency | 3 | Incremental ROIC well above WACC; warehouse lease model is asset-light |\n| Mix rationality | 3 | Dividend + buyback + organic reinvestment is coherent; dividend too high vs FCF |\n| Guidance accuracy | 2 | Management has beat revenue estimates; Q1 2026 beat; consistent |\n| **Total** | **10/15** | |\n| Insider bonus | +1 | CEO/insider ownership 58.08% [Actual \u2014 Aggregator]; no 6-mo net buy/sell data [Unconfirmed] |\n| **Adjusted total** | **11/15 \u2014 Good** | |\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS (v2.0)\n\n**Step 2.5-1. R&D capitalization:** R&D is immaterial for a packaging distributor (not separately reported). **[Assumption]**\n\n**Step 2.5-2. SBC treatment:** SBC = $1M (FY2025) [Actual]. Adjusted FCF = $33M - $1M = **$32M**. SBC is ~3% of NI \u2014 not a distortion.\n\n**Step 2.5-3. Operating lease capitalization:** Operating leases = $44M in FY2024 and FY2025 [Actual \u2014 filed]. Adjusted net debt:\n- Cash + short-term investments = $38M + $15.7M (Q2 2026 disclosed) = **$53.7M** [Actual]\n- Debt components: current portion $13M (FY2025) + operating leases $44M = **$57M** [Assumption \u2014 components may partly overlap per pack warning; vendor's total-debt scalar $44M suggests overlap, but even at $57M total]\n- **Adjusted net debt \u2248 $3M \u2192 net neutral.** No leverage issue either way.\n\n**Step 2.5-4. Brand/data assets:** KaratEarth eco line is a real brand but no Interbrand value is supportable. **[Assumption \u2014 no quantification]**\n\n**Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC:** Adjusting for R&D (none) and leases (add back rent \u2248 $6M [Estimate] to EBIT and $44M to IC): Adjusted ROIC = ($41M + $6M) \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.26) / ($157M equity + $57M debt \u2212 $54M cash + $44M leases \u2212 $6M amortized) \u2248 **$35M / $198M \u2248 17.7%**. GAAP ROIC \u2248 19\u201320%. Gap < 5pp \u2014 **no adjustment-driven conclusion change**.\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett):**\nOE = NI $31M + D&A $11M \u2212 maintenance capex $5M [Estimate] \u2212 \u0394WC $9M (inventory/receivables) = **$28M** [Estimate]\nAnd the dividend and buyback consume ~$45M/year (combined $37M + $8M) [Estimate]. **Owner earnings cover only ~62% of shareholder cash outflows.** This is the single most important normalizing insight in the file.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3. VALUATION ENGINE (Engine 1: Stable/Quality \u2014 DCF + Multiples; hybrid check with Engine 2)\n\n**Step 0 selection rationale (tie-back to Layer 0):**\n- **Reason 1**: Recurring, cash-generative distribution business \u2014 not an option story (no Engine 4).\n- **Reason 2**: Not a deep cyclical (no boom/bust capacity cycle; variable-cost heavy) \u2014 Engine 2 secondary only.\n- **Reason 3**: Growth is moderate (10%), margins stable \u2014 Engine 1 (DCF + multiples) is the right primary; we blend a cycle-adjusted sanity check because freight/tariff swings create 2\u20133yr earnings swings.\n- **Transition status**: None.\n\n### Forecast (explicit, per-year)\n\n**Revenue forecast [with sources]:**\n- FY2026: +11% \u2192 **$519M** (management guide \"low double-digit\", Q2 call) [Actual \u2014 guidance]\n- FY2027: +8% \u2192 **$561M** [Assumption \u2014 deceleration from chain ramp base effects; consistent with pack consensus +8.47% FY26 only]\n- FY2028: +7% \u2192 **$600M** [Assumption \u2014 industry ~4\u20135% + e-comm share gains]\n- FY2029: +6% \u2192 **$636M** [Assumption \u2014 fade toward industry + share gains]\n- FY2030: +5% \u2192 **$668M** [Assumption \u2014 mature growth]\n- Terminal: +3.5% (= long-run nominal GDP; below the 10Y of 4.7% - justified as the company's growth floor, consistent with a mature distributor) [Assumption]\n\n**Margin forecast:**\n- FY2026 EBIT margin: 8.8% (ex-refund, roughly flat to FY2025 \u2014 Q2 ex-refund GM 37.7% vs 39.5% prior-year reflects freight/resin pressure offset by sourcing gains) [Estimate]\n- FY2027: 9.2% [Assumption \u2014 freight normalizes, paper-bag scale, eco-mix]\n- FY2028: 9.5% [Assumption]\n- FY2029: 9.8% [Assumption]\n- FY2030: 10.0% [Assumption \u2014 long-run ceiling; competition prevents >10% EBIT in a distributor]\n- Terminal: 10.0% [Assumption]\n\n**D&A & capex:** D&A $11.5M growing ~4% [Arithmetic from FY25 $11.1M]; capex = $5M flat (vendor's broader figure, excludes growth-project step-up) [Assumption \u2014 no large expansion plan beyond Orlando warehouse lease; lease model keeps capex low]\n\n**Working capital:** \u0394WC = 19.4% of incremental revenue (= FY2025 WC $91.4M / revenue $468M) [Arithmetic]\n\n**Tax rate:** 26% (25% federal + state; using FY2025 NI/EBIT as