ed** EBITDA (~$75\u201378M including refunds): $825M\u20131.09B EV \u2192 $41\u201354/share. On ex-refund EBITDA (~$52M): $570\u2013730M \u2192 **$28\u201336/share**. \n- P/E on reported FY26 EPS $2.20: at 19\u00d7 (trailing) = $42; at 22\u00d7 = $48 \u2014 the market is at the top of the \"reported\" range.\n- **Reconciliation:** DCF ($41) + reported-EBITDA multiple ($41\u201354) + ex-refund multiple ($28\u201336) \u2192 the \"fair\" band is widest **$28\u201350**, central **$36\u201344**. We set IV = **$41** as center; at $49, the stock is 1.5\u00d7 the ex-refund fair value and ~0.95\u00d7 the reported-EBITDA top.\n\n**Reverse DCF:** At $49, the market implies: ~9% revenue CAGR with 10% terminal EBIT margin, or ~7% with 11% margins, OR a terminal multiple >20\u00d7. The first is achievable only if e-commerce sustains >25% growth and pricing holds \u2014 in a 3.4% inflation, 1.5% GDP world, **that is on the high side of feasible but not impossible** [Assumption].\n\n**Tornado (top 3 variables):**\n1. **WACC (Rf + ERP + beta)** \u2014 \u00b10.5pp changes IV by \u00b1~$5\n2. **Terminal EBIT margin** \u2014 9% vs 11% swings IV $6\u20138\n3. **Revenue CAGR (2027\u201330)** \u2014 \u00b12pp changes IV $4\u20136\n\n**These become monitoring KPIs (Section 8).**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n**Step 3.5-1. Competitive response:** If KRT's e-commerce/inbound model keeps taking share, competitors (Pactiv, Dart, Imperial Dade) can respond with (a) price cuts \u2014 DART already does direct; (b) Amazon marketplace competition \u2014 Amazon Basics disposable packaging could commoditize, a direct threat to 18.9% online channel; (c) freight war \u2014 larger players have better container contracts. **Impact: \u22122 (mildly negative).**\n\n**Step 3.5-2. Reflexivity:** Positive \u2014 high stock price enables SBC hiring, equity financing for warehouse/build-out, and customer trust for chain RFPs. Negative \u2014 a severe de-rating would not hurt the business itself (distributors don't rely on equity capital), so reflexivity is low. **+1.**\n\n**Step 3.5-3. Customer evolution:** Early adopters (independent restaurants, local chains) \u2192 mainstream (national chains: four new accounts in Q2, shipments Q4). As penetration grows, churn increases (chains do annual RFPs) and LTV/CAC for e-commerce worsens (rising online marketing costs). We assume online margins converge to chain margins over time. **[Assumption]**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\n**Prior (Layer 1.5):** Base rate for small-cap distributor sustaining >10% growth = 25\u201330%; base rate for dividend cut at >100% payout = 40\u201350%.\n\n**Bayesian update:**\nP(Success | volume beats, e-comm +37% July, chain wins, clean balance sheet) = Prior \u00d7 Likelihood / Norm.\nWe move the growth-sustainability prior from 27.5% to **35\u201340%** (new evidence), but keep the dividend-fix prior at 40% (unchanged \u2014 FCF coverage is weak).\n\n**Scenario distribution (total = 100%):**\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Thesis | 3-yr target | PV@10%, ~2.5y |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bull** | 20% | E-comm >25% sustained; GM 40%; EPS28 $2.30; 20\u00d7 | $46 | $36 |\n| **Base** | 50% | Revenue +8/6/5%; GM 37\u201338%; EPS28 $1.70; 17\u00d7 | $29 | $23 |\n| **Bear** | 30% | CBP penalty >$10M; freight/resin margin squeeze; dividend cut to $1.20; EPS28 $1.00; 13\u00d7 | $13 | $10 |\n\n**Expected 3-yr value: 0.2\u00d736 + 0.5\u00d723 + 0.3\u00d710 = $7.2 + $11.5 + $3.0 = $21.7**\n**Expected 3-yr return vs $49: \u221255.7%; annualized \u221222%.** \n\nNote: the scenario expected value is below the DCF IV of $41 because scenarios explicitly model de-rating risk, while a DCF terminal value presumes the multiple is permanently 12\u201318\u00d7. Both are discussed in the final decision reconciliation. The divergence is the **premium paid for optionality**, and it is negative at $49.\n\n**Base-case maximum probability = 50%** \u2713 (single highest).\n\n**Step 4-5. Expected price & MoS:** \nIV = $41 (mid). 50% CI: **$37\u201347**; 80% CI: **$31\u201353**. MoS @49: **\u221219.5%** \u2192 None.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\n**Market-implied horizon:** At $49, with FCF/share of $1.19 (FY2026e ex-refund), the market requires ~8.5% growth for 10 years to justify P/FCF of 41\u00d7 [Estimate]. The market is paying for ~2034 earnings. **Market horizon \u2248 8\u201310 years.**\n\n**My horizon:** 3\u20135 years. **Spread = \u22124 to \u22125 years \u2192 NO arbitrage; the market is pricing more than double my investment horizon.**\nIf the market horizon compresses to 5 years (as growth normalizes), fair value = $30\u201335.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\n**Step 5-1. Conviction score (15):**\n\n| Component | Score |\n|---|---|\n| Business quality | 7/10 \u2192 3/5 |\n| Valuation attractiveness | 2/5 (negative MoS) |\n| Macro alignment | 2/5 (headwind) |\n| Thesis clarity (KPIs) | 5/5 (transparent, measurable) |\n| Execution traceability | 5/5 (management beats guidance) |\n| **Total** | **17/25 \u2192 avg 3.4/5 \u2192 Medium (7/15 equiv.)** |\n| Post-decay half-life | 6 months \u2192 Low-Medium, **6.5/15** |\n\n**Step 5-2. Conviction decay:** no confirming evidence in last quarter beyond refunds; the normalized business did NOT improve (ex-refund GM **down** 180bp y/y). Decay applied \u2192 **6/15.**\n\n**Step 5-3. Sizing:** Medium/Low conviction \u2192 max 4\u20136%; valuation-adjusted and CBP-risk-adjusted \u2192 **0\u20132%** for new money. Existing positions: **trim to \u22642%.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n| Metric | Value | Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Short % float | 3.12%, \u221213.1% MoM [Actual] | Mildly bullish; shorts covering |\n| Days to cover | 2.06 [Actual] | No squeeze risk |\n| Institutional ownership | 37.11% [Actual] | Low sponsorship; under-owned |\n| Insider ownership | 58.08% [Actual] | High alignment; float small |\n| Dividend yield | 3.8\u20134.55% [Actual] | Income anchor; but payout > FCF |\n| Options positioning | n/a \u2014 no data in pack [Unconfirmed] | \u2014 |\n| Sentiment | Stock +138% off 52w low ($20.61\u2192$50.81) [Actual] | **Euphoric for a small-cap** |\n| Crowding risk | Price ABOVE mean analyst target ($41.5) [Actual] | **Crowded on the long side** |\n\n**Crowding risk score: 9/15 \u2192 position size \u22121%p.** No extreme short interest to fuel a squeeze; the tailwind from the 52-week breakout is likely spent.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\n- **Role**: not a macro hedge. High correlation to consumer spending (GDP slowdown hurts), fuel prices (negative), USD strength (positive), rates (negative via multiple).\n- In a 35% Reflation / 30% Stagflation / 20% Recession world, this is the **wrong place** \u2014 the stock behaves like a small-cap discretionary growth-equivalent, not like a defensive.\n- If already held: it provides a 3.8% dividend and potential 10%+ earnings growth, which can hedge a Goldilocks scenario (15% probability). It does not hedge the other 85%.\n- **Overlap risk**: any other import-dependent / consumer-discretionary / small-cap positions in the portfolio will amplify the KRT stress scenario (tariff shock, consumer slowdown).\n\n**Tail correlation:** In 2020 and 2022, small-cap consumer cyclicals sold off harder than the market. Assume **beta 1.2\u20131.4 in tail** (correlation > 0.7 with SPX on downside) [Assumption].\n\n**CVaR:** If the CBP investigation hits, the stock can plausibly lose 20\u201330% in a day; over a quarter, 40\u201360% drawdown is possible (modeled in the Bear scenario). Conditional 5% VaR \u2248 \u221255%.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\nEdge estimate, 12-month horizon:\n- Bull (+20%): prob 20%, gain $10\n- Base (0%): prob 50%, gain $4\n- Bear (\u221260%): prob 30%, loss $30\nExpected = 0.2\u00d710 + 0.5\u00d74 + 0.3\u00d7(\u221230) = **\u2212$5 \u2192 negative edge \u2192 Kelly fraction = 0%.**\n\n**No new money should be deployed at $49.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\n**Risks:**\n1. **CBP AD/CVD investigation** \u2014 potential penalties/back duties; company received $27M IEEPA refunds (Q2 transcript) \u2014 the refund may offset some old duties, but a fresh AD/CVD finding is a >$10M tail risk. **Probability: 15%** [Estimate]\n2. **Dividend coverage**: $1.84/sh cost \u2248 $37M vs ex-refund FCF \u2248 $32M \u2192 112% payout **Probability of a dividend cut within 24 months: 30\u201340%** [Estimate]\n3. **Freight/resin rally** \u2014 diesel $5.40, container rates +8.9% q/q \u2014 direct margin compression\n4. **E-commerce margin dilution** \u2014 online marketing + last-mile costs\n5. **Competition from Pactiv/Dart/Amazon Basics**\n\n**Catalysts:**\n1. Q3/Q4 2026 revenue acceleration (chain accounts ship; July online +37%)\n2. GM recovery to 40%+ as freight normalizes\n3. Resolution of CBP investigation with no penalty\n4. Dividend increase with sustainable FCF\n5. Continuation of buybacks ($10M authorization remaining)\n\n**Key event dates:** Q3 2026 earnings (Nov 2026), Q4/FY2026 earnings + guidance (Mar 2027), CBP ruling (unknown).\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\nThe common bear triggers across a portfolio holding KRT:\n- Oil spike (Iran war expansions) \u2192 freight + resin + fuel \u2192 hits KRT margins AND any other transport/consumer position.\n- Consumer slowdown (GDP 1.5% and falling) \u2192 hits all discretionary names.\n- Dollar strength \u2014 bull for KRT (import costs) but bear for multinationals; net mixed.\n\nIf any portfolio positions are in consumer discretionary, packaging, or logistics, the correlation-adjusted risk is elevated. KRT's idiosyncratic risk is NOT diversifiable away by \"more small-caps\" \u2014 it increases tail correlation.\n\n**Conclusion: KRT is a portfolio risk amplifier, not a diversifier, in the current macro.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 8. MONITORING & THESIS INTEGRITY (v2.0)\n\n**Thesis integrity KPIs \u2014 thresholds that trigger re-evaluation:**\n\n| KPI | Current | Trigger |\n|---|---|---|\n| E-commerce revenue growth | +23.6% Q2 / +37% July | <15% for 2 quarters \u2192 downgrade growth |\n| Ex-refund GM | 37.7% Q2 | <36% \u2192 thesis broken |\n| Adjusted EBITDA margin (ex-refund) | ~10\u201311% | <9% \u2192 thesis broken |\n| New chain accounts added | 4/quarter | 0 in two consecutive quarters |\n| Working capital / revenue | 19.4% | >22% \u2192 FCF conversion damaged |\n| Dividend coverage (FCF/div) | 0.88 | <0.75 \u2192 dividend cut risk high |\n| CBP case status | open | New penalty > $5M \u2192 re-run Bear scenario |\n\n**Pre-mortem \u2014 three ways this stock fails by 2031:**\n1. **\"The dividend was the product.\"** Yield investors buy at 4%; when the dividend is cut (because payout was 112% of FCF), the multiple de-rates from 19\u00d7 to 11\u00d7 in weeks. Warning: Q4 dividend announcement with no raise.\n2. **\"The AD/CVD handcuffs.\"** The CBP case results in a $20M penalty; the company loses China sourcing at the same time as freight peaks; GM compresses to 33%; earnings halve. Warning: CBP filing mentions \"expanded scope.\"\n3. **\"Amazon ate the channel.\"** E-commerce was 19% of sales; Amazon Basics disposable tableware + 3P sellers undercut KRT's online pricing; online growth turns negative; inventory write-downs follow. Warning: Amazon vendor-negotiation reports show share loss; online organic traffic flat for 2 quarters.\n\n**Decision journal entry (T0 = 2026-08-24):**\n- Price $49.00; IV $41; MoS \u221219.5%; action: TRIM/AVOID.\n- Key assumptions: WACC 9%, terminal g 3.5%, 10% EBIT margin ceiling, $5M capex.\n- Key red-team concerns: dividend payout > FCF, CBP tail, multiple above consensus target.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\n**Step 9-1. Short thesis (same data set):**\n1. **Normalized earnings are falling**: ex-refund GM 37.7% in Q2 2026 vs 39.5% prior year \u2014 the \"improvement\" is accounting, not operating.\n2. **The dividend is not covered**: $37M dividend vs $32M normalized FCF (and that FCF has capex understated 5.96\u00d7).\n3. **CBP AD/CVD case is open**: importer business model at risk; the $27M refund is a partial offset, not a clean resolution.\n4. **Analyst targets are below the price**: mean $41.5 vs $49 \u2014 the stock is trading 18% above the best-informed sell-side.\n5. **Valuation**: 36\u00d7 normalized EPS, 41\u00d7 normalized FCF for a 9% EBIT margin distributor.\n6. **Macro headwinds**: diesel $5.40/gal, container rates +8.9% q/q, US GDP growth 1.5% and slowing.\n7. **E-commerce margin dilution**: online fulfillment costs + marketing will compress the channel's contribution.\n\n**Step 9-2. Logic combat:**\n\n| Dimension | Long | Short |\n|---|---|---|\n| Earnings quality | 3/5 (refund noise) | 5/5 |\n| Growth evidence | 4/5 (chain adds, e-comm) | 2/5 (base effects) |\n| Margin trend | 3/5 (sourcing) | 4/5 (down y/y) |\n| Balance sheet | 5/5 | 4/5 (lease-adjusted) |\n| Valuation | 1/5 | 5/5 |\n| Regulatory | 2/5 | 4/5 |\n| **Total** | **18/30** | **24/30** |\n\nGap = 6 \u2192 **Short logic beats Long logic.** Application: Long conviction downgraded further.\n\n**Step 9-3. Adversarial data:** The research brief's \"William Blair PT $2,200\u20133,100\" is unrecoverable garbage \u2014 treat as [Unconfirmed/garbled]; Benzinga shows the price target set June 4, 2026, but the displayed $0.00 is also unreliable. We do not rely on it. No Hindenburg-style report exists for KRT. **[Unconfirmed]**\n\n**Step 9-4. Killed arguments:**\n- **\"Insider ownership 58% means the stock is safe.\"** Killed, because insiders can sell after lockup / in distributions; high insider ownership also means low public float \u2192 higher volatility on bad news. It remains valid as a governance signal only.\n- **\"The CBP case is immaterial.\"** Killed, because the pack's own risk-factor summary lists it prominently; the penalty range is unknown and could be material.\n- **\"E-comm growth validates the platform.\"** Killed in the long run, because Amazon can commoditize the channel; e-comm margin trends are not disclosed.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS (MANDATORY, v2.0)\n\n| Audit group | Pass/Fail/Notes |\n|---|---|\n| A. Methodology \u2014 DCF + multiples + scenario + base rates + Bayes | **PASS** |\n| B. Valuation \u2014 no single-method anchor; WACC derivation explicit; terminal g \u2264 3.5%; CI shown | **PASS** |\n| C. Bias \u2014 Overconfidence check: we actively reduced conviction after finding ex-refund GM down y/y and dividend >FCF. | **PASS** |\n| D. Consistency \u2014 No mixing of reported EBITDA and ex-refund FCF; no double-counting of refunds; TTM excludes refunds while guidance includes them \u2014 disclosed. | **PASS** |\n| E. Narrative \u2014 \"growth story\" was stress-tested against ex-refund margins. | **PASS** |\n| F. Red team \u2014 Short case articulates the fatal flaws; logic gap favors short \u2192 we downgrade. | **PASS** |\n| G. Data integrity \u2014 Vendor total debt ($44M) was NOT stacked on operating leases blindly; we noted the pack's overlap warning and used a conservative $57M total debt; capex discrepancy (5.96\u00d7) disclosed; William Blair garbled target not used; beta untrusted. | **PASS** |\n| *Base-rate shock* | Success \u2264 1.5\u00d7 base \u2014 no red flag |\n| *Layer 5 vs 6.5 gap* | Kelly says 0%; conviction says 0% \u2014 gap = 0. **PASS** |\n\n**37 Audit Points \u2014 all material points PASSED; no recalculation required.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n\n```\nTICKER: KRT\nT0: 2026-08-24 | PRICE: $49.00\nIV (base, mid): $41.00 | 80% CI: $31\u201353\nMoS: -19.5% (NONE)\nAction: TRIM/AVOID new money. Re-entry $34\u201338.\n\nKey inputs:\n- WACC: 9.0% (Rf 4.72% [Actual FRED], ERP 5.0%, beta 0.90)\n- FY26 revenue growth: +11% [Actual guidance]\n- FY27-30 revenue: +8/7/6/5% [Assumption]\n- Terminal EBIT margin: 10.0% [Assumption]\n- Terminal g: 3.5% [Assumption]\n- Capex: $5M/yr [Vendor-derived; filed=$1M]\n\nCritical red-team concerns:\n1. Dividend $37M vs ex-refund FCF $32M (112% payout)\n2. CBP AD/CVD case \u2014 unresolved, 15% tail probability\n3. Ex-refund GM DOWN 180bp y/y \u2014 operating story not improving\n4. Price above mean analyst target\n```\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 12. FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\n\n**Action: TRIM (if held) / AVOID (new money). Do not initiate at $49.**\n\n**Rationale in one line:** The stock has re-rated from $20.61 to $49.00 (+138%) on the back of one-time tariff refunds and low-float momentum, while the normalized operating business (ex-refund GM down, dividend uncovered, CBP case open) does not justify 36\u00d7 normalized EPS.\n\n**What changed from the beginning of the rally:** \n- Ex-refund gross margin compressed 180bp y/y in Q2 2026;\n- Analysts' mean target ($41.5) is 15% below the price;\n- Macro regime (oil shock, GDP 1.5%, 10Y at 4.7%) turned from neutral to headwind;\n- The dividend is the only remaining anchor, and it is at 112% of normalized FCF.\n\n**Strategy:**\n- **If you own it** (from lower levels): **Sell 50\u201370% here.** Hold a \u22642% core for the 3.8% dividend, the e-commerce option, and the clean balance sheet. Place a trailing stop at $42 (below the analyst mean).\n- **If you don't own it:** Do NOT chase. Place a limit order at **$34\u201338** (a 22\u201330% pullback), which equates to 17\u201319\u00d7 normalized FY27 EPS and a 5.2\u20135.9% FCF yield. At that level, initiate a **2\u20133% position**.\n- **Monitoring triggers to re-evaluate (Section 8):** ex-refund GM < 36%; e-comm growth < 15% for 2 quarters; dividend raise pause; CBP penalty announcement.\n\n**Position sizing: 0% new money; \u22642% if held (trim).** Sizing gap vs Layer 6.5 = 0% (consistent).\n\n**Confidence:** Low-Medium. The CBP investigation outcome and the path of freight/resin costs are the two variables that could make either the bull or bear case dominate. At $49, the risk/reward does not compensate for that uncertainty.\n