ally-driven moat; foodservice end-market is\nmodestly cyclical, but the business model resembles a stable, low-capex\ndistributor more than a commodity cyclical). [Assumption]\n\nValuation Engine: ENGINE 1 (Traditional DCF + Multiples).\nReasons:\n1. Revenue is contracted/order-based but recurring; history of 6 consecutive\n   fiscal years of positive operating income and positive OCF [Actual, SEC].\n2. Business model is distribution-led: low capex, high FCF conversion,\n   no binary optionality and no platform/installed-base monetization.\n3. Guidance exists for FY2026 only; a DCF with explicit fade is the correct\n   tool for valuing a slow-to-mid-growth distributor.\nErrors if alternative engine used:\n- Engine 4 (Option-Led) would overstate value by treating e-commerce\n  acceleration as a perpetual option; e-commerce is a channel, not a separate\n  high-optionality business.\n- Engine 3 (Hybrid) would attempt to value a \"platform\" that does not exist\n  (no recurring software/installed-base revenue).\nArchetype transition: NOT in transition. E-commerce >18% of sales and sourcing\ndiversification are mix shifts, not archetype changes. No Engine 3/4 blend\nwarranted. [Estimate]\n\nValuation Conclusion (summary): Base-case intrinsic value \u2248 $36.5/share\n(50% CI: $33\u2013$44; 80% CI: $26\u2013$55). Expected value across scenarios \u2248 $39.0.\nAt $49.00 the stock trades at a 20% premium to expected value and offers a\nnegative expected total return. Action: AVOID for new money; TRIM/REDUCE for\nholders.\n\nModel Confidence: MODERATE-LOW. High-quality Tier 1 financials [Actual] but\nheavy dependence on Tier 4/5 inputs: (a) CBP AD/CVD investigation outcome \u2014\n[Unconfirmed]; (b) post-2026 growth path \u2014 [Assumption]; (c) FCF\nnormalization after a one-time $25.8M IEEPA tariff refund \u2014 [Estimate].\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 1 \u2014 MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1-1. Macro Variable Check\n- 10Y UST: 4.65\u20134.74% (Aug 2026) [Actual \u2014 YCharts/FRED/TradingEconomics].\n- Real 10Y: \u2248 +1.7% (nominal 4.7% less core inflation \u22483.0%) [Estimate].\n- Fed funds: 3.50\u20133.75%, unchanged at July 2026 FOMC [Actual \u2014 Forbes];\n  stance: NEUTRAL (post-easing hold).\n- Gold: $4,580/oz [Actual \u2014 Yahoo Finance, Aug 2026]. This is a late-cycle\n  signal: elevated inflation/debasement hedging demand.\n- DXY: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not retrievable in budget]; qualitative read:\n  strong-USD tailwind for KRT as an importer.\n- Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed].\n- Economic cycle: LATE EXPANSION with stagflationary tail risk (high gold,\n  diesel $5.40/gal, Middle East instability).\n- Inflation: core \u22483.0% [Estimate]; headline elevated by energy.\n\nStep 1-2. Regime Classification (probabilities sum to 100%)\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 15%\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 35%\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 30%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 20%\n\nStep 1-3. Macro Sensitivity (axes; -3 to +3, negative = hurt by rising variable)\n- Interest rate sensitivity: -1 (low debt; modest consumer-delay exposure)\n- Dollar sensitivity: -1 (stronger USD reduces import COGS \u2014 an offset; but\n  the sign convention \"rising variable hurts\" makes this a net tailwind\n  that offsets part of rate/commodity pressure)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: -1 (small-cap, retail-influenced float)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: -1.5 (foodservice traffic is stable but not\n  recession-proof)\n- Commodity sensitivity: -2 (resin, diesel, ocean freight are direct inputs)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment\nRegime-weighted expected impact = NEUTRAL-to-HEADWIND.\n- 10Y at 4.7% compresses the fair multiple for a 27x-forward-earnings stock.\n- Diesel $5.40 and resin cost pressure directly hit COGS (management called\n  fuel costs the highest in a year) [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call].\n- Strong USD is a partial offset (Taiwan 46%, China 11% sourcing) [Actual].\nVerdict: Headwind tilt \u2192 Conviction -0.5 step.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE CHECK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rates (industry/size class) [Estimate \u2014 industry studies,\n    not company-specific]\n- 5-year survival of a profitable, 30-year-old distribution business: ~85%.\n- Probability that a 10% top-line grower sustains >7% growth beyond 5 years:\n  <30%.\n- Probability that a 27x-forward multiple is held when growth fades to 4\u20135%:\n  <20%.\n- Probability of material penalty/cash hit from a CBP AD/CVD investigation:\n  20\u201330% [Unconfirmed \u2014 non-public outcome].\n- Foodservice packaging industry real growth: ~3\u20135%/yr.\n\nStep 1.5-2. Company-Specific Deviation from Base Rates\nMy forecast deviates upward from the industry growth base rate (10%\u21924% fade\nvs 3\u20135% flat) for 3 reasons:\n1. E-commerce channel: +23.6% in Q2'26; >37% in July 2026; Amazon +49%\n   [Actual \u2014 company call]. This is a real channel shift, not a trend guess.\n2. Sourcing diversification: China reduced to 11% of sourcing, domestic to\n   ~20% [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call]; this creates a durable COGS advantage.\n3. New chain wins: 4 new chain accounts shipping in Q4'26; paper-bag contract\n   with a national chain worth ~$17M annualized [Actual \u2014 10-K excerpt/IR].\nThese justify modest deviation. They do NOT justify the market's implied\n>5 years of double-digit growth; my fade path remains below the consensus\n(+8.5% forward revenue per yfinance [Estimate]).\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check\nMy success probability (20% bull) is NOT >1.5x any applicable base rate.\nNo red flag triggered. If one assumed 50% probability of $65 bull \u2014 then\nsuccess probability >1.5x base rate \u2192 re-evaluation would be mandatory.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY (Buffett + Lynch)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences)\nKarat Packaging imports, converts, prints, and distributes disposable\nfoodservice packaging (cups, lids, containers, paper bags, eco-friendly\nKaratEarth products) to restaurant chains, distributors, and e-commerce\ncustomers, including Amazon. The company runs an extensive US warehouse and\ndelivery-fleet network and manages a diversified sourcing base (Taiwan 46%,\nChina 11%, domestic ~20%) [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call]. Its economics are those of a\nlow-capex distributor: GM ~37%, operating margin ~9%, minimal capital\nexpenditure.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Score (0\u20133 each)\n- Intangible assets (brand): 1 (KaratEarth emerging, not powerful)\n- Switching costs: 2 (chains value reliability; switching is disruptive)\n- Network effects: 0\n- Cost advantage: 2 (import-led scale vs smaller distributors)\n- Economies of scale: 2 (fleet + 15+ facilities)\n- Regulatory barriers: 0\n- Data moat: 0 (thin)\nTOTAL: 7/21 \u2192 NARROW MOAT.\nDirection: STABLE-to-WIDENING \u2014 evidence: online +23.6% [Actual]; eco\nproducts 33.8% of sales vs 31.8% [Actual]; domestic sourcing rising [Actual].\nOffset: Amazon is simultaneously a customer and a potential self-supplying\ncompetitor. [Estimate]\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification\nFAST GROWER (recent 10%+ growth) with Stalwart stability.\nCheckpoints: growth sustainability (fade expected \u2014 see Section 3);\ndebt (low \u2014 pass); margin stability (thin but stable \u2014 pass, with caution).\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\n- Revenue CAGR 5yr: +9.6% [Actual \u2014 arithmetic on FY20 $296M\u2192FY25 $468M]\n- Latest 4Q revenue growth (Q2'25\u2013Q1'26): \u2248 +11% [Estimate]\n- Gross margin FY25: 36.8% [Actual]; Q2'26 ex-refund: 37.7% [Actual]\n- Operating margin FY25: 8.8% [Actual]; 6-yr median: 8.9% [Actual]\n- Net margin FY25: 6.6% [Actual]\n- ROE (GAAP, FY25 equity): 31/157 = 19.7% [Arithmetic]; vendor ROE 30.6%\n  [Aggregator \u2014 period/definition different, likely includes one-time refund]\n- ROIC (estimated): NOPAT ~$30M / invested capital ~$176M \u2248 17% [Estimate]\n- FCF margin: FY25 7.1% [Actual]; FY24 11.1% [Actual]; normalized \u2248 8%\n  [Estimate]\n- Net debt/EBITDA: \u22480.06x (net debt \u2248$3M incl. operating leases) [Estimate]\n- Interest coverage: >20x [Estimate \u2014 FY23 interest $2M, EBIT $42M]\nJudgment: GOOD.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (per 15)\n1. Buyback efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 Q2'26 bought 73,510 shares at ~$27.20 [Arithmetic\n   \u2014 $2.0M/73,510] \u2014 astute price; but 3-yr share count CAGR +5.5% [Actual].\n2. M&A: 1/3 \u2014 history too thin to score higher [n/a \u2192 neutral].\n3. Capex efficiency: 3/3 \u2014 incremental ROIC far above WACC; asset-light\n   distribution.\n4. Rationality of mix: 2/3 \u2014 dividend + small buyback + minimal capex is\n   coherent; but dividend exceeds normalized FCF (see Section 2.5).\n5. Guidance accuracy: 2/3 \u2014 Q1'26 EPS beat ($0.34 vs $0.303) [Actual];\n   Q2'26 revenue beat ($136.3M vs $135.5M FactSet) [Actual].\nInsider bonus: 58.08% insider ownership [Aggregator] \u2192 +1 pt.\nTotal: 11/16 \u2192 GOOD. (Aligns with stewards, but dividend coverage is the\nweak point.)\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 2.5 \u2014 ADJUSTED FINANCIALS (Intangibles-Adjusted)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization\nR&D negligible for a distributor [Actual \u2014 no separate R&D line]. No\nadjustment. [Assumption]\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC Treatment\nSBC: FY21 $2M, FY22 $2M, FY23 $1M, FY24 $2M, FY25 $1M [Actual].\nAdj. owner FCF = reported FCF \u2212 SBC. FY25: $33M \u2212 $1M = $32M [Arithmetic].\nNo share-count explosion: FY22\u2013FY25 shares +1.3%, with FY2025 diluted count\nverified 20,180,070 [Actual].\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization\nFY2025 operating leases $44M; current portion of LT debt $13M; cash $38M;\nST investments $0 [Actual]. If leases are capitalized \u2192 adjusted net debt =\n$13 + $44 \u2212 $38 = $19M net debt (0.4x EBITDA) [Estimate, tag-dependent].\nIf leases are excluded \u2192 net cash $25M [Arithmetic]. We use the conservative\ninclusive figure: net debt \u2248 $3\u201319M; central $10M. (EV \u2248 $977\u2013987M.)\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/Data Assets\nKaratEarth brand value: [Unconfirmed \u2014 no Interbrand or equivalent].\nData assets: e-commerce analytics; not separately monetized [Assumption].\nNo adjustment added.\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\nAdjusted NOPAT \u2248 $30M [Estimate]; adjusted invested capital = equity $157M +\nnet debt $19M \u2212 cash/ST $0 \u2248 $176M [Estimate]; ROIC \u2248 17%.\nGAAP ROIC \u2248 15\u201317% \u2014 gap <5pp; no reliability concern.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (FY25)\nNI $31M + D&A $11M \u2212 maintenance capex $5M \u2212 \u0394WC (inflow $24M; normalize to\n$0) \u2212 SBC $1M \u2248 $36M [Estimate]. However, working capital normalization is\nuncertain: 2025 FCF of $33M included a $24M WC release [Arithmetic], so\nsustainable FCF is closer to $32\u201335M, not $40M+.\nKEY INSIGHT: Dividend $1.88/share annualized = $37.5M [Estimate] exceeds\nnormalized FCF ($32\u201335M) and FY25 FCF ($33M). Payout ratio on normalized FCF\n\u2248 107\u2013117% [Estimate]. This is the single most important bear flag.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nDiscount-Rate Derivation (WACC)\n- Risk-free = 10Y UST 4.70% [Actual]\n- ERP = 5.0% [Assumption \u2014 late-cycle, gold at $4,580, elevated uncertainty]\n- Beta = 0.85 [Assumption \u2014 vendor 0.791 [Aggregator] not used directly;\n  slight uplift for small-cap and energy-input exposure]\n- Cost of equity = 4.70 + 0.85 \u00d7 5.0 = 8.95%\n- Debt: pre-tax ~5.5\u20136.0% on leases/CP [Assumption]; after-tax \u2248 4.1\u20134.5%;\n  weight \u2248 3% [Estimate]\n- WACC = 8.8% \u2192 rounded to 9.0% (range 8.0\u201310.5% in sensitivity)\n\nExplicit Forecast (Base Case) \u2014 all $M\nForecast drivers \u2014 source each:\n- Revenue: FY26E $518M (+10.5% vs $468M) [Actual \u2014 company guidance\n  \"low-double-digit growth\"; Motley Fool transcript 2026-08-13].\n  FY27 +8.0%, FY28 +6.3%, FY29 +4.9%, FY30 +4.0% [Assumption \u2014 fade toward\n  industry growth base rate 3\u20135%; not established by any guidance beyond\n  FY26. If growth instead holds at 10%, add ~$4\u20136/share to IV; if it fades\n  to 2%, subtract ~$4\u20135/share.]\n- EBIT margin: FY26 9.3%, FY27 9.5%, FY28 9.6%, FY29 9.7%, FY30 9.8%\n  [Assumption \u2014 anchored on FY25 actual 8.8% and Q2'26 ex-refund GM 37.7%;\n  margin expansion only to 38% GM. Company guides \"mid-teens adjusted EBITDA\"\n  [Actual], which would imply ~12\u201313% EBITDA margin; my EBIT path is BELOW\n  company guidance \u2014 conservative. If mid-teens EBITDA materializes in 2H26,\n  add ~$6\u20138/share.]\n- D&A: FY26\u201330 $11.5M\u2192$13.5M [Assumption \u2014 FY25 actual $11M, growing with\n  modest PP&E; no company guidance beyond FY26]\n- Capex: FY26 $4M, FY27\u201330 $4.5\u20136.0M [Assumption \u2014 pack: $1M filed vs $5M\n  vendor; we use the mid-point because the $1M excludes software/intangibles\n  capex; not guided]\n- Change in working capital: FY26\u201330 $3.0M\u2192$2.0M use [Assumption \u2014 5-yr\n  arithmetic average \u2248 $5M/yr; offset by continued payables efficiency]\n- Tax rate: 25% [Assumption \u2014 federal+state; effective rate not reliably\n  reconstructable from pack, so a flat statutory assumption is used]\n\n| Year      | FY26E | FY27E | FY28E | 