 "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 428, "report": "\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 0. ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Ticker:** KRT (Karat Packaging, Inc.)\n**T0 Price:** $49.00 [Actual \u2014 vendor quote, 2026-08-24]\n**Market Cap:** $0.977B [Actual \u2014 vendor]\n**Analysis Date:** 2026-08-24\n**Analyst Coverage:** 3 analysts; Mean PT $41.5, Low $33 (BofA Sell), High $50 (Lake Street Buy) [Aggregator, 2026-08-15]\n**Base-Case Intrinsic Value:** $44.60 (50% CI: $39\u2013$52; 80% CI: $33\u2013$62)\n**Expected Value (probability-weighted):** $46.05\n**Margin of Safety at $49:** \u20139.9% (None)\n**Recommendation:** Do not initiate. Holders trim into $48\u2013$52. Re-entry: $33\u2013$38.\n\n**Data Quality Disclaimer:** The vendor's P/E of 19.16 is computed on earnings that include one-time IEEPA tariff refunds; the vendor's operating margin of 27.6% conflicts with filed operating margin of 8.8% and is rejected. Capex series has a $1M (filed) vs $5M (vendor) discrepancy; a rate of 1.2% of revenue is assumed. The CBP AD/CVD investigation status could not be secured before research budget exhaustion \u2014 marked [Unconfirmed] and carried into scenarios.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT ANALYSIS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1-1. Macro Variable Check**\n\n| Variable | Value | Direction | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10-Yr Treasury | 4.71% | +0.44 pt YoY, stable past month | Trading Economics [Actual, 2026-08-21] |\n| Fed Funds Target | 3.50%\u20133.75% | 5th consecutive hold; hike odds ~\u2153 at July meeting | U.S. Bank / Forbes [Actual] |\n| Real 10-Yr Rate | ~1.7% (4.71% less ~3% inflation) | Positive | [Estimate] |\n| Fed Stance | Neutral-to-Hawkish | Warsh holding on 2% target | U.S. Bank [Actual] |\n| Inflation | Elevated; energy-driven | Persisting | NTD/Fed minutes [Actual] |\n| Dollar (DXY context) | Strong vs TWD (CEO cites tailwind) | Strong USD | Company call [Actual] |\n| Credit Spreads | Not established in pack | \u2014 | [Unconfirmed] |\n| Regime | Reflation bias, stagflation tail risk | \u2014 | Judgment |\n\n**Step 1-2. Regime Probability Distribution**\n\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): **25%**\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): **40%**\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): **20%**\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): **15%**\n- Total = 100%.\n\nJustification: Fed funds 3.50\u20133.75% with a hawkish hold and a 10-yr at 4.71% argues against Goldilocks. Energy-driven inflation (diesel $5.40/gal; Middle East instability) plus resilience in US growth supports Reflation as modal, with Stagflation elevated.\n\n**Step 1-3. Macro Sensitivity (\u20143 to +3)**\n\n- Interest-rate sensitivity: **+1** (consumer cyclical packaging, but price-taker; moderate)\n- Dollar sensitivity: **+3** (46% Taiwan / 11% China sourcing; strong USD directly lowers import costs; CEO explicitly cited strong USD vs TWD as tailwind)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: **+1** (low debt, FCF-positive)\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity: **+1.5** (foodservice demand is cyclical but sticky)\n- Commodity sensitivity: **\u20132** (resin, diesel, container rates \u2014 Q2 2026: diesel $5.40; product costs 49.2% of sales vs 48.5% YoY)\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment**\n\nRegime-weighted expected impact = 0.25\u00d7(+) + 0.40\u00d7(+) + 0.20\u00d7(\u2212) + 0.15\u00d7(\u2212) \u2192 **Neutral to slight Headwind**. The strong dollar offsets some commodity-cost pressure, but the modal reflation regime with elevated energy costs pressure margins. No conviction adjustment, but attention to fuel/freight costs as thesis KPI. Headwind rules: strengthen split-entry discipline (not applicable at current no-initiate stance).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base Rates**\n\n- Stable incumbent packaging distributor, 5-year survival: **>90%** [Base rate \u2014 distribution businesses with neutral end-markets have high survival]\n- Probability of continued profitability (10-year track record of positive OI): **~85%** [Base rate]\n- Dividend sustainability for a company paying >90% of FCF: **~60%** [Base rate \u2014 dividend cuts cluster in first 3 years of a payout ratio >80%]\n- Platform/e-commerce transition (online 18.9% of sales) succeeding to >25% of sales: **~40%** [Base rate \u2014 omnichannel transitions fail roughly half the time in distribution]\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation Check**\n\nBase conclusion: fair but not cheap business. Base rate says a stable distributor at 27\u00d7 normalized earnings is a poor bargain. I require company-specific reasons to deviate above base rate on growth: (1) online channel growing 23.6% YoY (July +37%, Amazon +49%) \u2014 company-specific accelerator; (2) four new chain accounts ramping Q4 2026; (3) sourcing diversification (20% domestic, Taiwan 46%) reducing tariff exposure. These justify a growth rate slightly above industry, but NOT margins above history. No deviation >1.5\u00d7 base rate on success probability.\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check**\n\nNo variable exceeds the 1.5\u00d7 threshold. The bull case assumes e-commerce takes the company to >25% of sales and OI margins to ~12% \u2014 that is within 1.5\u00d7 of the 40% base rate for channel transitions.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences)**\n\nKarat Packaging is an import-led distributor/manufacturer of disposable foodservice packaging \u2014 cups, lids, containers, paper bags \u2014 sourcing primarily from Taiwan (46%), China (11%), and domestic (20%) suppliers, and selling to national restaurant chains, distributors, and an e-commerce channel (18.9% of sales; Amazon prominent). The company operates its own logistics network, including an internal delivery fleet and warehouses (new Orlando facility), which is its principal moat. It generates ~9\u201310% revenue growth, ~37% gross margins, ~9% operating margins, pays a $1.88/share dividend, and holds 58% insider ownership.\n\n**Understandability: High.**\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat Assessment**\n\n| Moat Type | Score (0\u20133) | Direction | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (KaratEarth brand) | 1 | Stable | Eco line growing 33.8% of sales but brand less recognized than Pactiv |\n| Switching costs | 2 | Stable | VMI, SKU-level integration, chain account relationships |\n| Network effects | 0 | \u2014 | None |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Narrowing | Direct-sourcing + fleet vs smaller rivals, but Pactiv/Berry have scale |\n| Economies of scale | 1 | Narrowing | Below critical scale vs $5B+ competitors |\n| Regulatory barriers | 1 | Stable | FDA food-contact standards; AD/CVD exposure is a risk, not a barrier |\n| Data moat | 1 | Widening | E-commerce/Amazon data, SKU-level demand signals |\n\n**Total: 8/21 \u2014 Narrow Moat. Direction: Stable-to-Narrowing.** The narrowing pressures come from: competitor scale (Pactiv Evergreen >$5B revenue), slower revenue growth than the competitor average (12.9% vs 27.5% \u2014 making that growth gap partly mix/coverage), and commoditized packaging logistics.\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch Classification: Stalwart** \u2014 steady 8\u201312% grower, consistent margins, dividend payer, modest cyclicality.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard**\n\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR (5-yr) | +9.6% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Revenue growth FY2025 | +10.7% | [Actual] |\n| Operating margin (FY2025 / 6-yr median) | 8.8% / 8.9% | [Arithmetic] |\n| ROE (vendor) | 30.6% | [Aggregator] |\n| ROIC (adjusted, see 2.5) | ~22.6% | [Estimate] |\n| FCF margin FY2025 | 7.1% (33/468) | [Arithmetic] |\n| FCF conversion vs OCF | 97% (33/34) | [Arithmetic] |\n| Net debt / EBITDA | Net cash ex-leases (~$0.7M net cash on 53.7 liquidity vs 57 debt incl. leases) | [Estimate] |\n| Interest coverage | >25\u00d7 (OI ~41 vs interest ~1) | [Estimate] |\n\n**Judgment: Good.** \u2014 but OCF declined from $47.6M (FY2024) to $33.7M (FY2025) primarily from tariff-inflated COGS; ex-refund margins have not recovered to FY2024 levels.\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard**\n\n1. **Buyback efficiency:** 2/3 \u2014 small but consistent repurchases ($2M in Q2 2026; $10M remaining); below-peak price? At $49 near 52-wk high: mixed.\n2. **M&A track record:** 3/3 \u2014 essentially no M&A; avoids integration risk.\n3. **Capex efficiency:** 3/3 \u2014 0.3% of revenue capex; incremental ROIC >100% on low invested base.\n4. **Rationality of mix:** 2/3 \u2014 dividend (3.9% yield) prioritized, likely FCF-constrained; could argue buyback better at $33 than $49.\n5. **Guidance accuracy:** 2/3 \u2014 FY2025 results met; Q2 2026 beat (136.3 vs 135.5 est.); but FY2026 GM guidance narrowed down from 33\u201335% band.\n   **Subtotal: 12/15.**\n   Insider: >5% insider ownership (58%) **+1**; no recent insider buying/selling data [Unconfirmed].\n   **Total: 13/16.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization:** Not applicable \u2014 R&D is immaterial for a packaging distributor.\n\n**Step 2.5-2. SBC Treatment:** FY2025 SBC = $1.0M [Actual]. Adjusted FCF = reported FCF $33.0M \u2212 SBC $1.0M = **$32.0M** [Arithmetic]. SBC is 2% of FCF \u2014 not a distortion.\n\n**Step 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization:** FY2025 operating leases = $44M [Actual]. Adjusted net debt = 0 (cash+ST inv 38.0+0) + current portion 13 + leases 44 \u2212 cash 38 \u2212 ST inv 0 = **$19M net debt**. Coverage remains strong. For EV, use $57M total debt (current portion 13 + leases 44).\n\n**Step 2.5-4. Brand/Data Assets:** KaratEarth line = 33.8% of sales (~$177M run-rate). Not independently valued. Data asset: e-commerce channel growing 23.6% \u2014 modest.\n\n**Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC**\n\n- Adjusted NOPAT = OI 41.0 \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.23) = $31.6M; + D&A 11 \u00d7 (1\u22120.23) = 8.5 \u2192 NOPAT = $40.1M (using EBITDA-level cash flow proxy).\n  Alternative direct: NOPAT = 31.6.\n- Adjusted Invested Capital = Equity 157 + Current Portion 13 + Op Leases 44 \u2212 Cash/ST Inv 38 = $176M.\n- **Adjusted ROIC \u2248 31.6/176 = 18.0%** (conservative) to 22.6% (excluding implicit financing). GAAP ROIC 23\u201324%. Gap <5pp \u2014 not material. [Estimate]\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett)**\n\nOwner Earnings = NI 31.5 + D&A 11.0 \u2212 Maintenance capex 1.0 \u2212 \u0394WC (\u221223.7) = **$65.2M** [Arithmetic, but \u0394WC was a source in FY2025 \u2014 not repeatable]. Normalized OE (ex-\u0394WC swing, using 15% of incremental revenue as WC growth): \u2248 **$38\u201340M**. [Estimate]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3. VALUATION ENGINE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 0-1/0-2/0-3: Enterprise Classification**\n\n**Archetype: A (Stable Incumbent) with transition traits toward B (Quality Compounder).**\nReasons: (1) 6-year continuous profitability with median OI margin 8.9%; (2) stable revenue growth 9.6% CAGR with low volatility; (3) asset-light distribution model producing high ROIC >18%; (4) dividend-payer with moderate leverage.\nNot yet Quality Compounder: ROIC > WACC only ~3 years; margin volatility (GM zig-zag 29.6%\u219241.2% vendor-adjusted) too high; tariff dependency unresolved.\n**Engine: Engine 1 \u2014 Traditional DCF + Multiples.** No blended engine needed; transition criteria <50% met.\n\n**Step 0-4: Engine Selection Rationale**\nAlternative engines rejected: Engine 2 (cyclical) inappropriate \u2014 margins are cost-driven, not demand-driven; Engine 3 (hybrid) inappropriate \u2014 no platform installed base; Engine 4/5 inappropriate \u2014 no optionality/binary event. Errors from misclassification: using an expectations-driven engine would value the tariff refunds as recurring; that is the index error of this market price.\n\n**Discount Rate Derivation**\n\n| Component | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free rate | 4.71% | 10-Yr UST, Trading Economics [Actual] |\n| ERP | 4.5% | [Assumption] |\n| Beta | 0.85 | Blend of vendor 0.79 [Aggregator] and sector/company fundamental beta (distribution, consumer-cyclical) [Assumption] |\n| Cost of equity (CAPM) | 4.71 + 0.85\u00d74.5 = **8.54%** | [Arithmetic] |\n| Pre-tax cost of debt | 6.0% | [Assumption] small private-like debt (op leases) |\n| Tax rate | 23% | FY2025: (41.0\u221231.5)/41.0 [Arithmetic] |\n| Equity weight | 94.5% (977 / (977+57)) | [Arithmetic] |\n| Debt weight | 5.5% | [Arithmetic] |\n| **WACC** | 0.945\u00d78.54 + 0.055\u00d76.0\u00d7(1\u22120.23) = **8.3%** | [Arithmetic] |\n\n**Explicit Forecast (All values $M; FYE December)**\n\n| | FY2025A | FY2026E | FY2027E | FY2028E | FY2029E | FY2030E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 468 | 524 | 568 | 610 | 653 | 697 |\n| Revenue growth | 10.7% | 12.0% | 8.5% | 7.5% | 7.0% | 6.7% |\n| Gross margin | 36.8% | 37.0% | 37.5% | 38.0% | 38.2% | 38.4% |\n| Gross profit | 172 | 194 | 213 | 232 | 249 | 268 |\n| Operating margin | 8.8% | 9.0% | 9.5% | 10.0% | 10.2% | 10.4% |\n| EBIT/OI | 41 | 47 | 54 | 61 | 67 | 72.5 |\n| D&A | 11 | 11.0 | 11.5 | 12.0 | 12.5 | 13.0 |\n| Capex (1.2% rev) | 1 | 6.3 | 6.8 | 7.3 | 7.8 | 8.4 |\n| \u0394WC (15% \u0394Rev) | \u221224 | 8.4 | 6.6 | 6.3 | 6.4 | 6.6 |\n| NOPAT (23% tax) | 31.6 | 36.3 | 41.6 | 47.0 | 51.3 | 55.8 |\n| FCFF | \u2014 | 32.6 | 39.7 | 45.4 | 49.6 | 53.8 |\n\n**Sourcing of forecast drivers:**\n- FY2026 revenue +12.0%: company guidance \"low double-digit\" [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 call] and consensus $523.9M [Aggregator \u2014 stockanalysis.com, S&P Global].\n- FY2027 revenue +8.5%: consensus $568.3M [Aggregator]. FY2028\u20132030 growth 7.5%\u21926.7%: my deceleration to terminal growth [Assumption] \u2014 not established by guidance.\n- Gross margin 37.0% FY2026: Q3 guide 35\u201337%, H1 2026 ex-refund average 36.9% [Actual/Estimate]. Rises to 38.4% by FY2030 [Assumption] \u2014 based on continued sourcing diversification (20% domestic now, Taiwan 46%); not guided beyond FY2026.\n- Operating margin 9.0%\u219210.4%: historical median 8.9%; assumes modest operating leverage [Assumption]. Not guided beyond FY2026 adjusted EBITDA \"mid-teens\" (which includes refunds).\n- Capex 1.2% of revenue: midpoint between filed ($1M; 0.2%) and vendor ($5M; 1.1%) [Assumption].\n- \u0394WC 15% of incremental revenue: historical \u0394WC/revenue step from FY2024\u2192FY2025 was 44% (treated as tariff-related distortion); FY2021\u2013FY2024 average = 19% [Arithmetic]. 15% is a conservative working assumption [Assumption].\n- Terminal growth 3% [Assumption \u2014 below long-run nominal GDP ~4%].\n- Tax 23% [Estimate \u2014 FY2025 arithmetic].\n\n**Terminal Value (Gordon):**\nTV = FCFF 2030 \u00d7 1.03 / (0.083 \u2212 0.03) = 55.4 / 0.053 = **$1,045M** [Arithmetic]\n\n**DCF Bridge ($M):**\nPV of explicit FCFF (mid-year discounting): 30.8 + 34.7 + 36.6 + 36.9 + 37.0 = **176.0**\nPV of terminal: 1,045 / (1.083^4.7) = **718.7**\nEnterprise Value: **894.7**\nPlus: Cash 38.0 + Short-term investments 15.7 (2025 had 0; 2024 had 28 \u2014 use 15.7 as current per Q2 call [Actual]) = 53.7\nLess: Total debt (current portion 13 + op leases 44) = 57.0\n**Equity Value: 891.4**\n**Per Share (20.0M shares): $44.60** [Estimate]\n\n**Sensitivity Table (IV per share):**\n\n| WACC \u2193 / Term g \u2192 | 2.0% | 3.0% | 3.5% |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7.8% | 49.0 | 54.1 | 58.4 |\n| 8.3% | 41.2 | 44.6 | 47.3 |\n| 9.0% | 34.0 | 36.3 | 37.9 |\n\n| OI margin scenario (WACC 8.3%, g 3%) \u2192 | 8.0% by FY2030 | 10.4% (base) | 12.0% by FY2028 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| IV | 34.1 | 44.6 | 55.3 |\n\n**Top 3 Tornado Variables:** 1) WACC (\u2192 IV 36\u201354), 2) Terminal growth (\u2192 IV 41\u201347), 3) FY2028\u201330 operating margin (\u2192 IV 34\u201355). These become thesis KPIs: fuel/freight costs, e-commerce mix, and long-duration rates.\n\n**Multiples Cross-Check:**\n- Normalized FY2026E EPS (ex-refund): ~$1.78 \u2192 market P/E at $49 = **27.5\u00d7** [Arithmetic]; at IV $44.60 = 25.1\u00d7.\n- Vendor P/E 19.2 on refund-inflated EPS: misleading; the $1.00/share Q2 2026 refund is non-recurring.\n- EV/EBITDA (FY2026E adjusted, ex-refund ~$58M): market EV 936\u2013980 / 58 = **16.1\u201316.9\u00d7**. Distribution comps (Veralto, Cintas-type) trade 12\u201316\u00d7; high end. [Assumption \u2014 no direct comps in pack]\n- P/B 5.73 [Aggregator] vs ROE 30.6% [Aggregator]: consistent with quality but not bargain.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n1. **Competitive response: \u22121.** If KRT's e-commerce acceleration (July +37%) persists, Pactiv Evergreen and Berry can undercut on price using superior scale; packaging distribution is commoditized at the margin. Risk of national-account price competition in 2027\u201328.\n2. **Reflexivity: +0.5.** The 3.9% dividend yield and buyback support the stock, which aids supplier confidence and customer trust. A dividend cut would reverse this.\n3. **Customer behavior: +1.** Shift from early-adopter to mainstream e-commerce adoption is a tailwind (Amazon +49% July). As chains consolidate SKUs, KRT's logistics moat becomes more valuable. But late-adopter customers may prefer large diversified suppliers \u2014 a headwind at the margin.\n   Net second-order: **\u22120.5** \u2014 roughly neutral.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 4-1/2. Scenarios (Probabilities sum to 100%)**\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Description | IV/share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bear | 25% | Stagflation/recession; GM compresses to 34%; OI margin 7%; growth 3\u20134%; CBP resolution adverse; dividend cut | $33.00 |\n| Base | 50% | Management executes: 12% growth FY2026, 7\u20138% thereafter; GM 37\u201338.4%; OI 9.0\u201310.4% | $44.60 |\n| Bull | 25% | E-commerce >25% of sales; GM 39%; OI 12% by FY2028; tariff environment normalizes; buyback accretive | $62.00 |\n\n**Step 4-3. Bayesian Update:**\nPrior (base rates, Layer 1.5): stable-distributor survival ~90%, dividend sustainability ~60%. Likelihood from Q2 2026: strong e-commerce beat (+23.6%), but ex-refund GM 37.7% down 180bp YoY, and Q2 ex-refund adjusted EBITDA $15.8M vs $17.7M prior-year. These update the posterior toward the Base case but reduce the Bull probability from 30% \u2192 25% and raise Bear from 20% \u2192 25%.\n\n**Step 4-4. Expected Price:**\nE[IV] = 0.25\u00d733 + 0.50\u00d744.6 + 0.25\u00d762 = **$46.05**\n50% CI: $39\u2013$52; 80% CI: $33\u2013$62.\n**MoS = (44.6 \u2212 49)/44.6 = \u22129.9% \u2192 None.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nMarket price of $49 implies: either (a) refund-boosted EPS of $2.72 recurs annually (P/E 18\u00d7), or (b) normalized EPS of $1.78 grows to ~$2.60 by 2028 and is valued at 19\u00d7. That is, the market's implied horizon and my 5-year horizon produce a **negative spread**: my IV $44.6 < price $49. The market is paying for the refund as if recurring; consensus FY2027 EPS is $2.33 (down 14.3% from FY2026E $2.72, per S&P Global via stockanalysis.com), confirming the market's own forecast is for a decline. No positive horizon arbitrage exists at T0.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550