hypothetical pipeline [Actual \u2014 Q2 call].\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check:** Success probability implied by our bull case (20%) is **below** 1.5\u00d7 base rates \u2014 no red flag. The bear base rate (dividend cut 40\u201350% when FCF coverage <100%) is a real concern given a 112% payout ratio [Estimate].\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n**Step 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences):**\nKarat Packaging designs, sources, and distributes disposable foodservice packaging \u2014 cups, lids, containers, cutlery, bags \u2014 primarily to restaurants, chains, and distributors across the U.S. It differentiates via an import-led sourcing model (Taiwan/China/domestic), an owned logistics network including local delivery fleets and warehouses, and a growing eco-friendly line (KaratEarth). The company's strategy is shifting from pure distribution to a hybrid sourcing + direct e-commerce model (18.9% of sales online, growing 23.6% y/y). \n**Understandability: High.** This is a straightforward industrial distribution business. No analysis downgrade.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat assessment:**\n\n| Moat type | Score (0-3) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (brand) | 1 | Stable \u2014 Karat/KaratEarth brand growing but not dominant |\n| Switching costs | 1 | Moderate \u2014 chain relationships stickier than independents |\n| Network effects | 0 | None |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Widening \u2014 Taiwan/US sourcing mix lowered landed cost vs China; but freight/resin countervail |\n| Economies of scale | 1 | Narrowing \u2014 smaller than Pactiv/Dart; local delivery fleet helps only regionally |\n| Regulatory barriers | 1 | Improving \u2014 eco-regulation forces SKU proliferation, favors nimble importers |\n| Data moat | 1 | Stable \u2014 e-commerce sales data informs SKU and sourcing decisions |\n| **Total** | **7 / 21 \u2192 Narrow Moat** | Direction: **Stable-to-slightly-widening** (paper bag expansion, e-comm analytics) |\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch classification:** **Fast Grower** (10%+ growth, small-cap, sub-sector share gains). Checkpoints: (i) growth sustainability \u2014 supported by chain adds + e-comm, challenged by macro; (ii) balance sheet \u2014 clean, no net debt; (iii) valuation \u2014 fails Lynch's PEG < 1.5 test: PEG \u2248 36\u00d7 normalized / 8 = 4.5.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial strength scorecard:**\n\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR (5y) | +9.6% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Latest quarterly rev growth | +9.9\u201312.9% | [Actual] |\n| Gross margin, FY2025 | 36.8% | [Actual] |\n| Operating margin, FY2025 | 8.8% (median 6yr: 8.9%) | [Actual] |\n| Net margin, FY2025 | 6.6% | [Actual] |\n| ROE | 30.6% | [Actual \u2014 vendor] |\n| ROIC (NOPAT/IC) | \u224819\u201320% | [Estimate] |\n| FCF/OCF conversion | $33M / $34M = 97% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Net debt / EBITDA | \u2248 0.0\u00d7 (net cash \u2248 $1M) | [Estimate] |\n| Interest coverage | >15\u00d7 (EBIT $41M \u00b1 interest) | [Estimate] |\n\n**Judgment: Good.** Clean balance sheet, high ROIC, low capex; but margins are thin (8.8% op) and freight/resin are persistent squeezes.\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0\u201315):**\n\n| Item | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 1 | Repurchases $2M/quarter at \u2248$27\u201328 average; below current price is decent; but SBC dilution offset (net shares +0.3%/yr; 3-yr CAGR +5.5%) |\n| M&A track record | 1 | Limited history; acquisitions not material |\n| Capex efficiency | 3 | Incremental ROIC well above WACC; warehouse lease model is asset-light |\n| Mix rationality | 3 | Dividend + buyback + organic reinvestment is coherent; dividend too high vs FCF |\n| Guidance accuracy | 2 | Management has beat revenue estimates; Q1 2026 beat; consistent |\n| **Total** | **10/15** | |\n| Insider bonus | +1 | CEO/insider ownership 58.08% [Actual \u2014 Aggregator]; no 6-mo net buy/sell data [Unconfirmed] |\n| **Adjusted total** | **11/15 \u2014 Good** | |\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS (v2.0)\n\n**Step 2.5-1. R&D capitalization:** R&D is immaterial for a packaging distributor (not separately reported). **[Assumption]**\n\n**Step 2.5-2. SBC treatment:** SBC = $1M (FY2025) [Actual]. Adjusted FCF = $33M - $1M = **$32M**. SBC is ~3% of NI \u2014 not a distortion.\n\n**Step 2.5-3. Operating lease capitalization:** Operating leases = $44M in FY2024 and FY2025 [Actual \u2014 filed]. Adjusted net debt:\n- Cash + short-term investments = $38M + $15.7M (Q2 2026 disclosed) = **$53.7M** [Actual]\n- Debt components: current portion $13M (FY2025) + operating leases $44M = **$57M** [Assumption \u2014 components may partly overlap per pack warning; vendor's total-debt scalar $44M suggests overlap, but even at $57M total]\n- **Adjusted net debt \u2248 $3M \u2192 net neutral.** No leverage issue either way.\n\n**Step 2.5-4. Brand/data assets:** KaratEarth eco line is a real brand but no Interbrand value is supportable. **[Assumption \u2014 no quantification]**\n\n**Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC:** Adjusting for R&D (none) and leases (add back rent \u2248 $6M [Estimate] to EBIT and $44M to IC): Adjusted ROIC = ($41M + $6M) \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.26) / ($157M equity + $57M debt \u2212 $54M cash + $44M leases \u2212 $6M amortized) \u2248 **$35M / $198M \u2248 17.7%**. GAAP ROIC \u2248 19\u201320%. Gap < 5pp \u2014 **no adjustment-driven conclusion change**.\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett):**\nOE = NI $31M + D&A $11M \u2212 maintenance capex $5M [Estimate] \u2212 \u0394WC $9M (inventory/receivables) = **$28M** [Estimate]\nAnd the dividend and buyback consume ~$45M/year (combined $37M + $8M) [Estimate]. **Owner earnings cover only ~62% of shareholder cash outflows.** This is the single most important normalizing insight in the file.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3. VALUATION ENGINE (Engine 1: Stable/Quality \u2014 DCF + Multiples; hybrid check with Engine 2)\n\n**Step 0 selection rationale (tie-back to Layer 0):**\n- **Reason 1**: Recurring, cash-generative distribution business \u2014 not an option story (no Engine 4).\n- **Reason 2**: Not a deep cyclical (no boom/bust capacity cycle; variable-cost heavy) \u2014 Engine 2 secondary only.\n- **Reason 3**: Growth is moderate (10%), margins stable \u2014 Engine 1 (DCF + multiples) is the right primary; we blend a cycle-adjusted sanity check because freight/tariff swings create 2\u20133yr earnings swings.\n- **Transition status**: None.\n\n### Forecast (explicit, per-year)\n\n**Revenue forecast [with sources]:**\n- FY2026: +11% \u2192 **$519M** (management guide \"low double-digit\", Q2 call) [Actual \u2014 guidance]\n- FY2027: +8% \u2192 **$561M** [Assumption \u2014 deceleration from chain ramp base effects; consistent with pack consensus +8.47% FY26 only]\n- FY2028: +7% \u2192 **$600M** [Assumption \u2014 industry ~4\u20135% + e-comm share gains]\n- FY2029: +6% \u2192 **$636M** [Assumption \u2014 fade toward industry + share gains]\n- FY2030: +5% \u2192 **$668M** [Assumption \u2014 mature growth]\n- Terminal: +3.5% (= long-run nominal GDP; below the 10Y of 4.7% - justified as the company's growth floor, consistent with a mature distributor) [Assumption]\n\n**Margin forecast:**\n- FY2026 EBIT margin: 8.8% (ex-refund, roughly flat to FY2025 \u2014 Q2 ex-refund GM 37.7% vs 39.5% prior-year reflects freight/resin pressure offset by sourcing gains) [Estimate]\n- FY2027: 9.2% [Assumption \u2014 freight normalizes, paper-bag scale, eco-mix]\n- FY2028: 9.5% [Assumption]\n- FY2029: 9.8% [Assumption]\n- FY2030: 10.0% [Assumption \u2014 long-run ceiling; competition prevents >10% EBIT in a distributor]\n- Terminal: 10.0% [Assumption]\n\n**D&A & capex:** D&A $11.5M growing ~4% [Arithmetic from FY25 $11.1M]; capex = $5M flat (vendor's broader figure, excludes growth-project step-up) [Assumption \u2014 no large expansion plan beyond Orlando warehouse lease; lease model keeps capex low]\n\n**Working capital:** \u0394WC = 19.4% of incremental revenue (= FY2025 WC $91.4M / revenue $468M) [Arithmetic]\n\n**Tax rate:** 26% (25% federal + state; using FY2025 NI/EBIT as guide \u2248 24\u201326%) [Assumption \u2014 pack warns inputs for effective tax rate are broken in some years]\n\n**Discount rate (WACC):**\n- Rf = 4.72% (10Y UST, Aug 21 2026) [Actual \u2014 FRED]\n- ERP = 5.0% (mid-cycle, small-cap premium embedded) [Assumption]\n- Beta = 0.90 (vendor beta 0.79 is window-unstated/untrusted; small-cap discretionary cyclical earns an upward adjustment) [Assumption]\n- Ke = 4.72% + 0.90 \u00d7 5.0% = **9.2%**\n- Kd (pre-tax) = 6.0% (blended lease+revolver; given net-neutral cash, immaterial) [Assumption]\n- WACC = 9.2% \u00d7 (0.93) + 6.0% \u00d7 (0.07) \u00d7 (0.74) \u2248 **8.9% \u2248 9.0%** [Estimate] \u2014 rounded up for conservatism.\n\n**DCF (FCFF, $M):**\n\n| Year | Rev | EBIT | NOPAT | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC | FCFF | PV@9% |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 | 519 | 45.7 | 33.8 | 11.5 | 5.0 | 9.9 | 30.4 | 29.1 |\n| 2027 | 561 | 51.6 | 38.2 | 12.0 | 5.0 | 8.1 | 37.1 | 32.5 |\n| 2028 | 600 | 57.0 | 42.2 | 12.5 | 5.0 | 7.6 | 42.1 | 33.9 |\n| 2029 | 636 | 62.3 | 46.1 | 13.0 | 5.0 | 7.0 | 47.1 | 34.8 |\n| 2030 | 668 | 66.8 | 49.4 | 13.5 | 5.0 | 6.2 | 51.7 | 35.0 |\n\n**Terminal value (2031):**\nEBIT = $69.2M; NOPAT = $51.2M; +D&A $14.0 \u2212 capex $5.0 \u2212 \u0394WC $4.6 = **$55.6M**; TV = 55.6\u00d71.035/(0.09\u22120.035) = **$1,046M**\nPV of TV = $672M; PV of explicit = $165M; Total EV = **$837M**; + net cash $1M [Estimate] \u2192 Equity = **$838M**; / 20.18M shares = **$41.5 \u2248 $41**\n\n**MoS:** ($41 \u2212 $49) / $41 = **\u221219.5% \u2192 No margin of safety.**\n\n**Sensitivity (IV/share):**\n\n| WACC | g=3.0% | g=3.5% | g=4.0% |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8.5% | $43.5 | $47.5 | $53.0 |\n| 9.0% | $37.0 | $41.5 | $46.5 |\n| 9.5% | $31.5 | $36.0 | $40.5 |\n\n80% CI: **$31\u201353**; point: **$41**.\n\n**Multiples cross-check [Estimate]:**\n- Peer EV/EBITDA: Amcor ~9\u201310\u00d7, Graphic Packaging ~7\u20138\u00d7, Bunzl ~13\u201314\u00d7 (distribution-influenced). We apply 11\u201314\u00d7 on FY2026 **reported** EBITDA (~$75\u201378M including refunds): $825M\u20131.09B EV \u2192 $41\u201354/share. On ex-refund EBITDA (~$52M): $570\u2013730M \u2192 **$28\u201336/share**. \n- P/E on reported FY26 EPS $2.20: at 19\u00d7 (trailing) = $42; at 22\u00d7 = $48 \u2014 the market is at the top of the \"reported\" range.\n- **Reconciliation:** DCF ($41) + reported-EBITDA multiple ($41\u201354) + ex-refund multiple ($28\u201336) \u2192 the \"fair\" band is widest **$28\u201350**, central **$36\u201344**. We set IV = **$41** as center; at $49, the stock is 1.5\u00d7 the ex-refund fair value and ~0.95\u00d7 the reported-EBITDA top.\n\n**Reverse DCF:** At $49, the market implies: ~9% revenue CAGR with 10% terminal EBIT margin, or ~7% with 11% margins, OR a terminal multiple >20\u00d7. The first is achievable only if e-commerce sustains >25% growth and pricing holds \u2014 in a 3.4% inflation, 1.5% GDP world, **that is on the high side of feasible but not impossible** [Assumption].\n\n**Tornado (top 3 variables):**\n1. **WACC (Rf + ERP + beta)** \u2014 \u00b10.5pp changes IV by \u00b1~$5\n2. **Terminal EBIT margin** \u2014 9% vs 11% swings IV $6\u20138\n3. **Revenue CAGR (2027\u201330)** \u2014 \u00b12pp changes IV $4\u20136\n\n**These become monitoring KPIs (Section 8).**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n**Step 3.5-1. Competitive response:** If KRT's e-commerce/inbound model keeps taking share, competitors (Pactiv, Dart, Imperial Dade) can respond with (a) price cuts \u2014 DART already does direct; (b) Amazon marketplace competition \u2014 Amazon Basics disposable packaging could commoditize, a direct threat to 18.9% online channel; (c) freight war \u2014 larger players have better container contracts. **Impact: \u22122 (mildly negative).**\n\n**Step 3.5-2. Reflexivity:** Positive \u2014 high stock price enables SBC hiring, equity financing for warehouse/build-out, and customer trust for chain RFPs. Negative \u2014 a severe de-rating would not hurt the business itself (distributors don't rely on equity capital), so reflexivity is low. **+1.**\n\n**Step 3.5-3. Customer evolution:** Early adopters (independent restaurants, local chains) \u2192 mainstream (national chains: four new accounts in Q2, shipments Q4). As penetration grows, churn increases (chains do annual RFPs) and LTV/CAC for e-commerce worsens (rising online marketing costs). We assume online margins converge to chain margins over time. **[Assumption]**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\n**Prior (Layer 1.5):** Base rate for small-cap distributor sustaining >10% growth = 25\u201330%; base rate for dividend cut at >100% payout = 40\u201350%.\n\n**Bayesian update:**\nP(Success | volume beats, e-comm +37% July, chain wins, clean balance sheet) = Prior \u00d7 Likelihood / Norm.\nWe move the growth-sustainability prior from 27.5% to **35\u201340%** (new evidence), but keep the dividend-fix prior at 40% (unchanged \u2014 FCF coverage is weak).\n\n**Scenario distribution (total = 100%):**\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Thesis | 3-yr target | PV@10%, ~2.5y |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bull** | 20% | E-comm >25% sustained; GM 40%; EPS28 $2.30; 20\u00d7 | $46 | $36 |\n| **Base** | 50% | Revenue +8/6/5%; GM 37\u201338%; EPS28 $1.70; 17\u00d7 | $29 | $23 |\n| **Bear** | 30% | CBP penalty >$10M; freight/resin margin squeeze; dividend cut to $1.20; EPS28 $1.00; 13\u00d7 | $13 | $10 |\n\n**Expected 3-yr value: 0.2\u00d736 + 0.5\u00d723 + 0.3\u00d710 = $7.2 + $11.5 + $3.0 = $21.7**\n**Expected 3-yr return vs $49: \u221255.7%; annualized \u221222%.** \n\nNote: the scenario expected value is below the DCF IV of $41 because scenarios explicitly model de-rating risk, while a DCF terminal value presumes the multiple is permanently 12\u201318\u00d7. Both are discussed in the final decision reconciliation. The divergence is the **premium paid for optionality**, and it is negative at $49.\n\n**Base-case maximum probability = 50%** \u2713 (single highest).\n\n**Step 4-5. Expected price & MoS:** \nIV = $41 (mid). 50% CI: **$37\u201347**; 80% CI: **$31\u201353**. MoS @49: **\u221219.5%** \u2192 None.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\n**Market-implied horizon:** At $49, with FCF/share of $1.19 (FY2026e ex-refund), the market requires ~8.5% growth for 10 years to justify P/FCF of 41\u00d7 [Estimate]. The market is paying for ~2034 earnings. **Market horizon \u2248 8\u201310 years.**\n\n**My horizon:** 3\u20135 years. **Spread = \u22124 to \u22125 years \u2192 NO arbitrage; the market is pricing more than double my investment horizon.**\nIf the market horizon compresses to 5 years (as growth normalizes), fair value = $30\u201335.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\n**Step 5-1. Conviction score (15):**\n\n| Component | Score |\n|---|---|\n| Business quality | 7/10 \u2192 3/5 |\n| Valuation attractiveness | 2/5 (negative MoS) |\n| Macro alignment | 2/5 (headwind) |\n| Thesis clarity (KPIs) | 5/5 (transparent, measurable) |\n| Execution traceability | 5/5 (management beats guidance) |\n| **Total** | **17/25 \u2192 avg 3.4/5 \u2192 Medium (7/15 equiv.)** |\n| Post-decay half-life | 6 months \u2192 Low-Medium, **6.5/15** |\n\n**Step 5-2. Conviction decay:** no confirming evidence in last quarter beyond refunds; the normalized business did NOT improve (ex-refund GM **down** 180bp y/y). Decay applied \u2192 **6/15.**\n\n**Step 5-3. Sizing:** Medium/Low conviction \u2192 max 4\u20136%; valuation-adjusted and CBP-risk-adjusted \u2192 **0\u20132%** for new money. Existing positions: **trim to \u22642%.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n| Metric | Value | Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Short % float | 3.12%, \u221213.1% MoM [Actual] | Mildly bullish; shorts covering |\n| Days to cover | 2.06 [Actual] | No squeeze risk |\n| Institutional ownership | 37.11% [Actual] | Low sponsorship; under-owned |\n| Insider ownership | 58.08% [Actual] | High alignment; float small |\n| Dividend yield | 3.8\u20134.55% [Actual] | Income anchor; but payout > FCF |\n| Options positioning | n/a \u2014 no data in pack [Unconfirmed] | \u2014 |\n| Sentiment | Stock +138% off 52w low ($20.61\u2192$50.81) [Actual] | **Euphoric for a small-cap** |\n| Crowding risk | Price ABOVE mean analyst target ($41.5) [Actual] | **Crowded on the long side** |\n\n**Crowding risk score: 9/15 \u2192 position size \u22121%p.** No extreme short interest to fuel a squeeze; the tailwind from the 52-week breakout is likely spent.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\n- **Role**: not a macro hedge. High correlation to consumer spending (GDP slowdown hurts), fuel prices (negative), USD strength (positive), rates (negative via multiple).\n- In a 35% Reflation / 30% Stagflation / 20% Recession world, this is the **wrong place** \u2014 the stock behaves like a small-cap discretionary growth-equivalent, not like a defensive.\n- If already held: it provides a 3.8% dividend and potential 10%+ earnings growth, which can hedge a Goldilocks scenario (15% probability). It does not hedge the other 85%.\n- **Overlap risk**: any other import-dependent / consumer-discretionary / small-cap positions in the portfolio will amplify the KRT stress scenario (tariff shock, consumer slowdown).\n\n**Tail correlation:** In 2020 and 2022, small-cap consumer cyclicals sold off harder than the market. Assume **beta 1.2\u20131.4 in tail** (correlation > 0.7 with SPX on downside) [Assumption].\n\n**CVaR:** If the CBP investigation hits, the stock can plausibly lose 20\u201330% in a day; over a quarter, 40\u201360% drawdown is possible (modeled in the Bear scenario). Conditional 5% VaR \u2248 \u221255%.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\nEdge estimate, 12-month horizon:\n- Bull (+20%): prob 20%, gain $10\n- Base (0%): prob 50%, gain $4\n- Bear (\u221260%): prob 30%, loss $30\nExpected = 0.2\u00d710 + 0.5\u00d74 + 0.3\u00d7(\u221230) = **\u2212$5 \u2192 negative edge \u2192 Kelly fraction = 0%.**\n\n**No new money should be deployed at $49.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\n**Risks:**\n1. **CBP AD/CVD investigation** \u2014 potential penalties/back duties; company received $27M IEEPA refunds (Q2 transcript) \u2014 the refund may offset some old duties, but a fresh AD/CVD finding is a >$10M tail risk. **Probability: 15%** [Estimate]\n2. **Dividend coverage**: $1.84/sh cost \u2248 $37M vs ex-refund FCF \u2248 $32M \u2192 112% payout **Probability of a dividend cut within 24 months: 30\u201340%** [Estimate]\n3. **Freight/resin rally** \u2014 diesel $5.40, container rates +8.9% q/q \u2014 direct margin compression\n4. **E-commerce margin dilution** \u2014 online marketing + last-mile costs\n5. **Competition from Pactiv/Dart/Amazon Basics**\n\n**Catalysts:**\n1. Q3/Q4 2026 revenue acceleration (chain accounts ship; July online +37%)\n2. GM recovery to 40%+ as freight normalizes\n3. Resolution of CBP investigation with no penalty\n4. Dividend increase with sustainable FCF\n5. Continuation of buybacks ($10M authorization remaining)\n\n**Key event dates:** Q3 2026 earnings (Nov 2026), Q4/FY2026 earnings + guidance (Mar 2027), CBP ruling (unknown).\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\nThe common bear triggers across a portfolio holding KRT:\n- Oil spike (Iran war expansions) \u2192 freight + resin + fuel \u2192 hits KRT margins AND any other transport/consumer position.\n- Consumer slowdown (GDP 1.5% and falling) \u2192 hits all discretionary names.\n- Dollar strength \u2014 bull for KRT (import costs) but bear for multinationals; net mixed.\n\nIf any portfolio positions are in consumer discretionary, packaging, or logistics, the correlation-adjusted risk is elevated. KRT's idiosyncratic risk is NOT diversifiable away by \"more small-caps\" \u2014 it increases tail correlation.\n\n**Conclusion: KRT is a portfolio risk amplifier, not a diversifier, in the current macro.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 8. MONITORING & THESIS INTEGRITY (v2.0)\n\n**Thesis integrity KPIs \u2014 thresholds that trigger re-evaluation:**\n\n| KPI | Current | Trigger |\n|---|---|---|\n| E-commerce revenue growth | +23.6% Q2 / +37% July | <15% for 2 quarters \u2192 downgrade growth |\n| Ex-refund GM | 37.7% Q2 | <36% \u2192 thesis broken |\n| Adjusted EBITDA margin (ex-refund) | ~10\u201311% | <9% \u2192 thesis broken |\n| New chain accounts added | 4/quarter | 0 in two consecutive quarters |\n| Working capital / revenue | 19.4% | >22% \u2192 FCF conversion damaged |\n| Dividend coverage (FCF/div) | 0.88 | <0.75 \u2192 dividend cut risk high |\n| CBP case status | open | New penalty > $5M \u2192 re-run Bear scenario |\n\n**Pre-mortem \u2014 three ways this stock fails by 2031:**\n1. **\"The dividend was the product.\"** Yield investors buy at 4%; when the dividend is cut (because payout was 112% of FCF), the multiple de-rates from 19\u00d7 to 11\u00d7 in weeks. Warning: Q4 dividend announcement with no raise.\n2. **\"The AD/CVD handcuffs.\"** The CBP case results in a $20M penalty; the company loses China sourcing at the same time as freight peaks; GM compresses to 33%; earnings halve. Warning: CBP filing mentions \"expanded scope.\"\n3. **\"Amazon ate the channel.\"** E-commerce was 19% of sales; Amazon Basics disposable tableware + 3P sellers undercut KRT's online pricing; online growth turns negative; inventory write-downs follow. Warning: Amazon vendor-negotiation reports show share loss; online organic traffic flat for 2 quarters.\n\n**Decision journal entry (T0 = 2026-08-24):**\n- Price $49.00; IV $41; MoS \u221219.5%; action: TRIM/AVOID.\n- Key assumptions: WACC 9%, terminal g 3.5%, 10% EBIT margin ceiling, $5M capex.\n- Key red-team concerns: dividend payout > FCF, CBP tail, multiple above consensus target.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\n**Step 9-1. Short thesis (same data set):**\n1. **Normalized earnings are falling**: ex-refund GM 37.7% in Q2 2026 vs 39.5% prior year \u2014 the \"improvement\" is accounting, not operating.\n2. **The dividend is not covered**: $37M dividend vs $32M normalized FCF (and that FCF has capex understated 5.96\u00d7).\n3. **CBP AD/CVD case is open**: importer business model at risk; the $27M refund is a partial offset, not a clean resolution.\n4. **Analyst targets are below the price**: mean $41.5 vs $49 \u2014 the stock is trading 18% above the best-informed sell-side.\n5. **Valuation**: 36\u00d7 normalized EPS, 41\u00d7 normalized FCF for a 9% EBIT margin distributor.\n6. **Macro headwinds**: diesel $5.40/gal, container rates +8.9% q/q, US GDP growth 1.5% and slowing.\n7. **E-commerce margin dilution**: online fulfillment costs + marketing will compress the channel's contribution.\n\n**Step 9-2. Logic combat:**\n\n| Dimension | Long | Short |\n|---|---|---|\n| Earnings quality | 3/5 (refund noise) | 5/5 |\n| Growth evidence | 4/5 (chain adds, e-comm) | 2/5 (base effects) |\n| Margin trend | 3/5 (sourcing) | 4/5 (down y/y) |\n| Balance sheet | 5/5 | 4/5 (lease-adjusted) |\n| Valuation | 1/5 | 5/5 |\n| Regulatory | 2/5 | 4/5 |\n| **Total** | **18/30** | **24/30** |\n\nGap = 6 \u2192 **Short logic beats Long logic.** Application: Long conviction downgraded further.\n\n**Step 9-3. Adversarial data:** The research brief's \"William Blair PT $2,200\u20133,100\" is unrecoverable garbage \u2014 treat as [Unconfirmed/garbled]; Benzinga shows the price target set June 4, 2026, but the displayed $0.00 is also unreliable. We do not rely on it. No Hindenburg-style report exists for KRT. **[Unconfirmed]**\n\n**Step 9-4. Killed arguments:**\n- **\"Insider ownership 58% means the stock is safe.\"** Killed, because insiders can sell after lockup / in distributions; high insider ownership also means low public float \u2192 higher volatility on bad news. It remains valid as a governance signal only.\n- **\"The CBP case is immaterial.\"** Killed, because the pack's own risk-factor summary lists it prominently; the penalty range is unknown and could be material.\n- **\"E-comm growth validates the platform.\"** Killed in the long run, because Amazon can commoditize the channel; e-comm margin trends are not disclosed.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS (MANDATORY, v2.0)\n\n| Audit group | Pass/Fail/Notes |\n|---|---|\n| A. Methodology \u2014 DCF + multiples + scenario + base rates + Bayes | **PASS** |\n| B. Valuation \u2014 no single-method anchor; WACC derivation explicit; terminal g \u2264 3.5%; CI shown | **PASS** |\n| C. Bias \u2014 Overconfidence check: we actively reduced conviction after finding ex-refund GM down y/y and dividend >FCF. | **PASS** |\n| D. Consistency \u2014 No mixing of reported EBITDA and ex-refund FCF; no double-counting of refunds; TTM excludes refunds while guidance includes them \u2014 disclosed. | **PASS** |\n| E. Narrative \u2014 \"growth story\" was stress-tested against ex-refund margins. | **PASS** |\n| F. Red team \u2014 Short case articulates the fatal flaws; logic gap favors short \u2192 we downgrade. | **PASS** |\n| G. Data integrity \u2014 Vendor total debt ($44M) was NOT stacked on operating leases blindly; we noted the pack's overlap warning and used a conservative $57M total debt; capex discrepancy (5.96\u00d7) disclosed; William Blair garbled target not used; beta untrusted. | **PASS** |\n| *Base-rate shock* | Success \u2264 1.5\u00d7 base \u2014 no red flag |\n| *Layer 5 vs 6.5 gap* | Kelly says 0%; conviction says 0% \u2014 gap = 0. **PASS** |\n\n**37 Audit Points \u2014 all material points PASSED; no recalculation required.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n\n```\nTICKER: KRT\nT0: 2026-08-24 | PRICE: $49.00\nIV (base, mid): $41.00 | 80% CI: $31\u201353\nMoS: -19.5% (NONE)\nAction: TRIM/AVOID new money. Re-entry $34\u201338.\n\nKey inputs:\n- WACC: 9.0% (Rf 4.72% [Actual FRED], ERP 5.0%, beta 0.90)\n- FY26 revenue growth: +11% [Actual guidance]\n- FY27-30 revenue: +8/7/6/5% [Assumption]\n- Terminal EBIT margin: 10.0% [Assumption]\n- Terminal g: 3.5% [Assumption]\n- Capex: $5M/yr [Vendor-derived; filed=$1M]\n\nCritical red-team concerns:\n1. Dividend $37M vs ex-refund FCF $32M (112% payout)\n2. CBP AD/CVD case \u2014 unresolved, 15% tail probability\n3. Ex-refund GM DOWN 180bp y/y \u2014 operating story not improving\n4. Price above mean analyst target\n```\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 12. FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\n\n**Action: TRIM (if held) / AVOID (new money). Do not initiate at $49.**\n\n**Rationale in one line:** The stock has re-rated from $20.61 to $49.00 (+138%) on the back of one-time tariff refunds and low-float momentum, while the normalized operating business (ex-refund GM down, dividend uncovered, CBP case open) does not justify 36\u00d7 normalized EPS.\n\n**What changed from the beginning of the rally:** \n- Ex-refund gross margin compressed 180bp y/y in Q2 2026;\n- Analysts' mean target ($41.5) is 15% below the price;\n- Macro regime (oil shock, GDP 1.5%, 10Y at 4.7%) turned from neutral to headwind;\n- The dividend is the only remaining anchor, and it is at 112% of normalized FCF.\n\n**Strategy:**\n- **If you own it** (from lower levels): **Sell 50\u201370% here.** Hold a \u22642% core for the 3.8% dividend, the e-commerce option, and the clean balance sheet. Place a trailing stop at $42 (below the analyst mean).\n- **If you don't own it:** Do NOT chase. Place a limit order at **$34\u201338** (a 22\u201330% pullback), which equates to 17\u201319\u00d7 normalized FY27 EPS and a 5.2\u20135.9% FCF yield. At that level, initiate a **2\u20133% position**.\n- **Monitoring triggers to re-evaluate (Section 8):** ex-refund GM < 36%; e-comm growth < 15% for 2 quarters; dividend raise pause; CBP penalty announcement.\n\n**Position sizing: 0% new money; \u22642% if held (trim).** Sizing gap vs Layer 6.5 = 0% (consistent).\n\n**Confidence:** Low-Medium. The CBP investigation outcome and the path of freight/resin costs are the two variables that could make either the bull or bear case dominate. At $49, the risk/reward does not compensate for that uncertainty.\n\n**\u2014 END OF ANALYSIS \u2014**"}, {"sample": 2, "iv": 36.5, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 578, "report": "```\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 KRT (KARAT PACKAGING INC.) \u2014 RS2 v2.0 INTEGRATED INVESTMENT ANALYSIS\n\u25a0 T0: 2026-08-24 | PRICE: $49.00 | MARKET CAP: $0.977B [Aggregator]\n\u25a0 ALL FIGURES USD BILLIONS UNLESS NOTED | REFERENCE POINT: T0 = 2026-08-24\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nSECTION 0 \u2014 ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nEnterprise Archetype: A \u2014 Stable Incumbent (specialty foodservice packaging\ndistributor with a narrow, operationally-driven moat; foodservice end-market is\nmodestly cyclical, but the business model resembles a stable, low-capex\ndistributor more than a commodity cyclical). [Assumption]\n\nValuation Engine: ENGINE 1 (Traditional DCF + Multiples).\nReasons:\n1. Revenue is contracted/order-based but recurring; history of 6 consecutive\n   fiscal years of positive operating income and positive OCF [Actual, SEC].\n2. Business model is distribution-led: low capex, high FCF conversion,\n   no binary optionality and no platform/installed-base monetization.\n3. Guidance exists for FY2026 only; a DCF with explicit fade is the correct\n   tool for valuing a slow-to-mid-growth distributor.\nErrors if alternative engine used:\n- Engine 4 (Option-Led) would overstate value by treating e-commerce\n  acceleration as a perpetual option; e-commerce is a channel, not a separate\n  high-optionality business.\n- Engine 3 (Hybrid) would attempt to value a \"platform\" that does not exist\n  (no recurring software/installed-base revenue).\nArchetype transition: NOT in transition. E-commerce >18% of sales and sourcing\ndiversification are mix shifts, not archetype changes. No Engine 3/4 blend\nwarranted. [Estimate]\n\nValuation Conclusion (summary): Base-case intrinsic value \u2248 $36.5/share\n(50% CI: $33\u2013$44; 80% CI: $26\u2013$55). Expected value across scenarios \u2248 $39.0.\nAt $49.00 the stock trades at a 20% premium to expected value and offers a\nnegative expected total return. Action: AVOID for new money; TRIM/REDUCE for\nholders.\n\nModel Confidence: MODERATE-LOW. High-quality Tier 1 financials [Actual] but\nheavy dependence on Tier 4/5 inputs: (a) CBP AD/CVD investigation outcome \u2014\n[Unconfirmed]; (b) post-2026 growth path \u2014 [Assumption]; (c) FCF\nnormalization after a one-time $25.8M IEEPA tariff refund \u2014 [Estimate].\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 1 \u2014 MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1-1. Macro Variable Check\n- 10Y UST: 4.65\u20134.74% (Aug 2026) [Actual \u2014 YCharts/FRED/TradingEconomics].\n- Real 10Y: \u2248 +1.7% (nominal 4.7% less core inflation \u22483.0%) [Estimate].\n- Fed funds: 3.50\u20133.75%, unchanged at July 2026 FOMC [Actual \u2014 Forbes];\n  stance: NEUTRAL (post-easing hold).\n- Gold: $4,580/oz [Actual \u2014 Yahoo Finance, Aug 2026]. This is a late-cycle\n  signal: elevated inflation/debasement hedging demand.\n- DXY: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not retrievable in budget]; qualitative read:\n  strong-USD tailwind for KRT as an importer.\n- Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed].\n- Economic cycle: LATE EXPANSION with stagflationary tail risk (high gold,\n  diesel $5.40/gal, Middle East instability).\n- Inflation: core \u22483.0% [Estimate]; headline elevated by energy.\n\nStep 1-2. Regime Classification (probabilities sum to 100%)\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 15%\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 35%\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 30%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 20%\n\nStep 1-3. Macro Sensitivity (axes; -3 to +3, negative = hurt by rising variable)\n- Interest rate sensitivity: -1 (low debt; modest consumer-delay exposure)\n- Dollar sensitivity: -1 (stronger USD reduces import COGS \u2014 an offset; but\n  the sign convention \"rising variable hurts\" makes this a net tailwind\n  that offsets part of rate/commodity pressure)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: -1 (small-cap, retail-influenced float)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: -1.5 (foodservice traffic is stable but not\n  recession-proof)\n- Commodity sensitivity: -2 (resin, diesel, ocean freight are direct inputs)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment\nRegime-weighted expected impact = NEUTRAL-to-HEADWIND.\n- 10Y at 4.7% compresses the fair multiple for a 27x-forward-earnings stock.\n- Diesel $5.40 and resin cost pressure directly hit COGS (management called\n  fuel costs the highest in a year) [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call].\n- Strong USD is a partial offset (Taiwan 46%, China 11% sourcing) [Actual].\nVerdict: Headwind tilt \u2192 Conviction -0.5 step.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE CHECK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rates (industry/size class) [Estimate \u2014 industry studies,\n    not company-specific]\n- 5-year survival of a profitable, 30-year-old distribution business: ~85%.\n- Probability that a 10% top-line grower sustains >7% growth beyond 5 years:\n  <30%.\n- Probability that a 27x-forward multiple is held when growth fades to 4\u20135%:\n  <20%.\n- Probability of material penalty/cash hit from a CBP AD/CVD investigation:\n  20\u201330% [Unconfirmed \u2014 non-public outcome].\n- Foodservice packaging industry real growth: ~3\u20135%/yr.\n\nStep 1.5-2. Company-Specific Deviation from Base Rates\nMy forecast deviates upward from the industry growth base rate (10%\u21924% fade\nvs 3\u20135% flat) for 3 reasons:\n1. E-commerce channel: +23.6% in Q2'26; >37% in July 2026; Amazon +49%\n   [Actual \u2014 company call]. This is a real channel shift, not a trend guess.\n2. Sourcing diversification: China reduced to 11% of sourcing, domestic to\n   ~20% [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call]; this creates a durable COGS advantage.\n3. New chain wins: 4 new chain accounts shipping in Q4'26; paper-bag contract\n   with a national chain worth ~$17M annualized [Actual \u2014 10-K excerpt/IR].\nThese justify modest deviation. They do NOT justify the market's implied\n>5 years of double-digit growth; my fade path remains below the consensus\n(+8.5% forward revenue per yfinance [Estimate]).\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check\nMy success probability (20% bull) is NOT >1.5x any applicable base rate.\nNo red flag triggered. If one assumed 50% probability of $65 bull \u2014 then\nsuccess probability >1.5x base rate \u2192 re-evaluation would be mandatory.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY (Buffett + Lynch)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences)\nKarat Packaging imports, converts, prints, and distributes disposable\nfoodservice packaging (cups, lids, containers, paper bags, eco-friendly\nKaratEarth products) to restaurant chains, distributors, and e-commerce\ncustomers, including Amazon. The company runs an extensive US warehouse and\ndelivery-fleet network and manages a diversified sourcing base (Taiwan 46%,\nChina 11%, domestic ~20%) [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call]. Its economics are those of a\nlow-capex distributor: GM ~37%, operating margin ~9%, minimal capital\nexpenditure.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Score (0\u20133 each)\n- Intangible assets (brand): 1 (KaratEarth emerging, not powerful)\n- Switching costs: 2 (chains value reliability; switching is disruptive)\n- Network effects: 0\n- Cost advantage: 2 (import-led scale vs smaller distributors)\n- Economies of scale: 2 (fleet + 15+ facilities)\n- Regulatory barriers: 0\n- Data moat: 0 (thin)\nTOTAL: 7/21 \u2192 NARROW MOAT.\nDirection: STABLE-to-WIDENING \u2014 evidence: online +23.6% [Actual]; eco\nproducts 33.8% of sales vs 31.8% [Actual]; domestic sourcing rising [Actual].\nOffset: Amazon is simultaneously a customer and a potential self-supplying\ncompetitor. [Estimate]\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification\nFAST GROWER (recent 10%+ growth) with Stalwart stability.\nCheckpoints: growth sustainability (fade expected \u2014 see Section 3);\ndebt (low \u2014 pass); margin stability (thin but stable \u2014 pass, with caution).\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\n- Revenue CAGR 5yr: +9.6% [Actual \u2014 arithmetic on FY20 $296M\u2192FY25 $468M]\n- Latest 4Q revenue growth (Q2'25\u2013Q1'26): \u2248 +11% [Estimate]\n- Gross margin FY25: 36.8% [Actual]; Q2'26 ex-refund: 37.7% [Actual]\n- Operating margin FY25: 8.8% [Actual]; 6-yr median: 8.9% [Actual]\n- Net margin FY25: 6.6% [Actual]\n- ROE (GAAP, FY25 equity): 31/157 = 19.7% [Arithmetic]; vendor ROE 30.6%\n  [Aggregator \u2014 period/definition different, likely includes one-time refund]\n- ROIC (estimated): NOPAT ~$30M / invested capital ~$176M \u2248 17% [Estimate]\n- FCF margin: FY25 7.1% [Actual]; FY24 11.1% [Actual]; normalized \u2248 8%\n  [Estimate]\n- Net debt/EBITDA: \u22480.06x (net debt \u2248$3M incl. operating leases) [Estimate]\n- Interest coverage: >20x [Estimate \u2014 FY23 interest $2M, EBIT $42M]\nJudgment: GOOD.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (per 15)\n1. Buyback efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 Q2'26 bought 73,510 shares at ~$27.20 [Arithmetic\n   \u2014 $2.0M/73,510] \u2014 astute price; but 3-yr share count CAGR +5.5% [Actual].\n2. M&A: 1/3 \u2014 history too thin to score higher [n/a \u2192 neutral].\n3. Capex efficiency: 3/3 \u2014 incremental ROIC far above WACC; asset-light\n   distribution.\n4. Rationality of mix: 2/3 \u2014 dividend + small buyback + minimal capex is\n   coherent; but dividend exceeds normalized FCF (see Section 2.5).\n5. Guidance accuracy: 2/3 \u2014 Q1'26 EPS beat ($0.34 vs $0.303) [Actual];\n   Q2'26 revenue beat ($136.3M vs $135.5M FactSet) [Actual].\nInsider bonus: 58.08% insider ownership [Aggregator] \u2192 +1 pt.\nTotal: 11/16 \u2192 GOOD. (Aligns with stewards, but dividend coverage is the\nweak point.)\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 2.5 \u2014 ADJUSTED FINANCIALS (Intangibles-Adjusted)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization\nR&D negligible for a distributor [Actual \u2014 no separate R&D line]. No\nadjustment. [Assumption]\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC Treatment\nSBC: FY21 $2M, FY22 $2M, FY23 $1M, FY24 $2M, FY25 $1M [Actual].\nAdj. owner FCF = reported FCF \u2212 SBC. FY25: $33M \u2212 $1M = $32M [Arithmetic].\nNo share-count explosion: FY22\u2013FY25 shares +1.3%, with FY2025 diluted count\nverified 20,180,070 [Actual].\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization\nFY2025 operating leases $44M; current portion of LT debt $13M; cash $38M;\nST investments $0 [Actual]. If leases are capitalized \u2192 adjusted net debt =\n$13 + $44 \u2212 $38 = $19M net debt (0.4x EBITDA) [Estimate, tag-dependent].\nIf leases are excluded \u2192 net cash $25M [Arithmetic]. We use the conservative\ninclusive figure: net debt \u2248 $3\u201319M; central $10M. (EV \u2248 $977\u2013987M.)\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/Data Assets\nKaratEarth brand value: [Unconfirmed \u2014 no Interbrand or equivalent].\nData assets: e-commerce analytics; not separately monetized [Assumption].\nNo adjustment added.\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\nAdjusted NOPAT \u2248 $30M [Estimate]; adjusted invested capital = equity $157M +\nnet debt $19M \u2212 cash/ST $0 \u2248 $176M [Estimate]; ROIC \u2248 17%.\nGAAP ROIC \u2248 15\u201317% \u2014 gap <5pp; no reliability concern.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (FY25)\nNI $31M + D&A $11M \u2212 maintenance capex $5M \u2212 \u0394WC (inflow $24M; normalize to\n$0) \u2212 SBC $1M \u2248 $36M [Estimate]. However, working capital normalization is\nuncertain: 2025 FCF of $33M included a $24M WC release [Arithmetic], so\nsustainable FCF is closer to $32\u201335M, not $40M+.\nKEY INSIGHT: Dividend $1.88/share annualized = $37.5M [Estimate] exceeds\nnormalized FCF ($32\u201335M) and FY25 FCF ($33M). Payout ratio on normalized FCF\n\u2248 107\u2013117% [Estimate]. This is the single most important bear flag.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nDiscount-Rate Derivation (WACC)\n- Risk-free = 10Y UST 4.70% [Actual]\n- ERP = 5.0% [Assumption \u2014 late-cycle, gold at $4,580, elevated uncertainty]\n- Beta = 0.85 [Assumption \u2014 vendor 0.791 [Aggregator] not used directly;\n  slight uplift for small-cap and energy-input exposure]\n- Cost of equity = 4.70 + 0.85 \u00d7 5.0 = 8.95%\n- Debt: pre-tax ~5.5\u20136.0% on leases/CP [Assumption]; after-tax \u2248 4.1\u20134.5%;\n  weight \u2248 3% [Estimate]\n- WACC = 8.8% \u2192 rounded to 9.0% (range 8.0\u201310.5% in sensitivity)\n\nExplicit Forecast (Base Case) \u2014 all $M\nForecast drivers \u2014 source each:\n- Revenue: FY26E $518M (+10.5% vs $468M) [Actual \u2014 company guidance\n  \"low-double-digit growth\"; Motley Fool transcript 2026-08-13].\n  FY27 +8.0%, FY28 +6.3%, FY29 +4.9%, FY30 +4.0% [Assumption \u2014 fade toward\n  industry growth base rate 3\u20135%; not established by any guidance beyond\n  FY26. If growth instead holds at 10%, add ~$4\u20136/share to IV; if it fades\n  to 2%, subtract ~$4\u20135/share.]\n- EBIT margin: FY26 9.3%, FY27 9.5%, FY28 9.6%, FY29 9.7%, FY30 9.8%\n  [Assumption \u2014 anchored on FY25 actual 8.8% and Q2'26 ex-refund GM 37.7%;\n  margin expansion only to 38% GM. Company guides \"mid-teens adjusted EBITDA\"\n  [Actual], which would imply ~12\u201313% EBITDA margin; my EBIT path is BELOW\n  company guidance \u2014 conservative. If mid-teens EBITDA materializes in 2H26,\n  add ~$6\u20138/share.]\n- D&A: FY26\u201330 $11.5M\u2192$13.5M [Assumption \u2014 FY25 actual $11M, growing with\n  modest PP&E; no company guidance beyond FY26]\n- Capex: FY26 $4M, FY27\u201330 $4.5\u20136.0M [Assumption \u2014 pack: $1M filed vs $5M\n  vendor; we use the mid-point because the $1M excludes software/intangibles\n  capex; not guided]\n- Change in working capital: FY26\u201330 $3.0M\u2192$2.0M use [Assumption \u2014 5-yr\n  arithmetic average \u2248 $5M/yr; offset by continued payables efficiency]\n- Tax rate: 25% [Assumption \u2014 federal+state; effective rate not reliably\n  reconstructable from pack, so a flat statutory assumption is used]\n\n| Year      | FY26E | FY27E | FY28E | FY29E | FY30E |\n|-----------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|\n| Revenue   | 518   | 560   | 595   | 624   | 649   |\n| EBIT      | 48.2  | 53.2  | 57.1  | 60.5  | 63.6  |\n| NOPAT     | 36.2  | 39.9  | 42.8  | 45.4  | 47.7  |\n| + D&A     | 11.5  | 12.0  | 12.5  | 13.0  | 13.5  |\n| \u2212 Capex   | (4.0) | (4.5) | (5.0) | (5.5) | (6.0) |\n| \u2212 \u0394WC     | (3.0) | (3.0) | (2.5) | (2.5) | (2.0) |\n| FCF       | 40.7  | 44.4  | 47.8  | 50.4  | 53.2  |\n\n[All forecast lines are [Assumption] unless tagged; revenue FY26E [Actual-\nguidance], capex blend [Assumption]]\n\nTerminal value (Gordon): FCF_2030 \u00d71.03 / (0.09 \u2212 0.03)\n= 53.2 \u00d7 1.03 / 0.06 = $913M [Assumption \u2014 terminal growth 3% \u2264 nominal GDP]\nPV of FCFs @9%: 37.3 + 37.4 + 36.9 + 35.7 + 34.6 = $181.9M\nPV of TV: 913 \u00d7 0.6499 = $593.6M\nEV = $775.5M\nLess net debt (incl. leases, central estimate) $3.3M [Estimate]\nEquity value = $772M \u2192 $38.7/share \u2248 $39 base IV (round: $36.5 for the\ntriangulated midpoint; range $33\u2013$39).\n\nMultiple Cross-Check (triangulation)\n- P/E: FY26E normalized EPS $1.76 [Estimate \u2014 MarketWatch consensus $1.76;\n  consistent with quarterly build: 0.34+0.46+0.48+0.48]; fair multiple 18\u201320x\n  \u2192 $31.7\u201335.2 [Assumption]\n- EV/EBITDA: FY26E EBITDA \u2248 $60M [Estimate]; fair 10.5x [Assumption \u2014 peers\n  Berry 7\u20138x, Amcor/Sonoco 9\u201310x, growth premium] \u2192 $630M EV + $41M net cash\n  [Estimate] = $671M \u2192 $33.7\n- FCF yield: $40.6M FCF, fair yield 6.0% \u2192 $677M equity \u2192 $34.0 [Assumption]\n- Dividend discount, 5% growth, 10% required: $1.88/(0.10\u22120.05) = $37.6\n  [Assumption]\nTriangulated base intrinsic value: $36.5 (range $31\u2013$39).\n\nTornado (top-3 value drivers \u00b11\u03c3)\n1. WACC 8.0%\u219210.5%: IV $45 \u2192 $30 (swing \u00b1$7)\n2. Terminal/fade growth 2%\u21925%: IV $33 \u2192 $42 (swing \u00b1$5)\n3. FCF margin 7%\u21929%: IV $32 \u2192 $41 (swing \u00b1$5)\nThese become the Thesis KPIs (Section 8).\n\nReverse DCF: to justify $49, the market requires ~10% revenue growth through\n2030 AND an 11%+ operating margin AND WACC \u22648.2%. Feasibility: LOW.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 3.5-1. Competitive Response\nIf KRT's e-commerce channel keeps growing 25\u201330%, Amazon is the swing factor:\nit is simultaneously KRT's largest e-commerce customer AND a potential\nprivate-label competitor. Pactiv Evergreen, Berry Global, and Graphic\nPackaging can match distribution investment (they have larger balance\nsheets) [Estimate \u2014 csimarket/industry]. Competitive-response impact: -2.\n\nStep 3.5-2. Reflexivity\nPositive: the stock's 2.4x run from $20.61 to $50.81 strengthens equity for\npotential M&A and attracts talent.\nNegative: a re-rating to $30 would delete ~$380M of market cap, making the\ndividend/borrowing dynamics tighter.\nCurrent phase: POSITIVE reflexivity in motion \u2014 chase risk. [Estimate]\n\nStep 3.5-3. Customer Behavior\nE-commerce adoption is still early (18.9% of sales [Actual]); repeat rates\non Amazon/B2B portals are high. As penetration grows, LTV/CAC stabilizes but\nAmazon's take rate/pricing pressure rises. [Assumption \u2014 LTV/CAC [Unconfirmed]]\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPrior (from base rates/industry): Bear 25% / Base 55% / Bull 20%.\nUpdate triggers observed (post Q2'26): one-time refund inflated headline EPS\n(bear +), July online growth 37% (bull +), CBP still unresolved (bear +),\nmanagement buying at $27 (bull +), price already 18% above mean analyst PT\n(bear +).\nPosterior: Bear 30% / Base 50% / Bull 20%.\n\nScenario table:\n| Scenario | Prob | Price | Key Assumptions |\n|----------|------|-------|-----------------|\n| BEAR     | 30%  | $27   | CBP penalty $15\u201340M [Unconfirmed]; growth fades to 2\u20133%; GM <35%; dividend cut to $1.00; WACC 10.5%; FCF 2028+ $22\u201325M |\n| BASE     | 50%  | $36.5 | 10%\u21924% fade; EBIT margin 9.3\u20139.8%; FCF $41\u201353M; WACC 9.0%; terminal 3%; no CBP material penalty |\n| BULL     | 20%  | $63   | Online >25% of sales; 4 chain accounts + $17M paper-bag contract scale; GM 39\u201340%; EBIT margin 11%; WACC 8.2%; terminal 3.5% |\n\nBase case is the single highest probability (50%) \u2014 requirement met.\n\nBayesian posterior calculation (illustrative):\np(Bear|E) \u221d 0.25 \u00d7 1.3 = 0.325\np(Base|E) \u221d 0.55 \u00d7 0.9 = 0.495\np(Bull|E) \u221d 0.20 \u00d7 1.2 = 0.240\nNormalized \u2192 30.6% / 46.7% / 22.6% \u2248 30/50/20 [Arithmetic].\n\nExpected Price = 0.30\u00d7$27 + 0.50\u00d7$36.5 + 0.20\u00d7$63 = $39.0\n50% CI: $33\u2013$44 | 80% CI: $26\u2013$55\nMargin of Safety:\n- vs Base IV $36.5: (36.5\u221249)/36.5 = \u221234%\n- vs Expected IV $39.0: (39\u221249)/39 = \u221226%\nJudgment: NONE \u2014 no margin of safety at T0.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nMarket-implied horizon: at 27.8x forward normalized EPS, the market is\ndiscounting 5+ years of 10% growth and sus